WEBVTT

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.090
<v Kirk Johnson>Ajmal, can you hear me?
<v Kirk Johnson>Ajmal，你能听到我说话吗？

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:19.670
<v Ajmal>Hello?
<v Ajmal>喂？

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:20.000
<v Kirk Johnson>Hello?
<v Kirk Johnson>喂？

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.240
<v Ajmal>Yes, I can hear you.
<v Ajmal>能，我能听到。

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.380
<v Kirk Johnson>OK, so are you still in front of the Canadian or not?
<v Kirk Johnson>好的，你现在还在那个加拿大人前面吗？

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:30.510
<v Ajmal>Yeah, in front of the Canadian, in the water.
<v Ajmal>在的，在加拿大人前面，在水里。

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.610
<v Kirk Johnson>You're in the water right now?
<v Kirk Johnson>你现在在水里？

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:34.650
<v Ajmal>Yeah.
<v Ajmal>对。

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:47.660
<v David Kestenbaum>This is a phone call between a man in California and a man standing in a canal outside the airport in Kabul, Afghanistan. That'll all make more sense in a minute. We had a totally different show planned for you today.
<v David Kestenbaum>这是一个住在加利福尼亚州的男人与一个站在阿富汗喀布尔机场外运河里的男人之间的通话。过一会儿你们就会明白是怎么回事了。我们原本为今天安排了一档完全不同的节目。

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:57.260
<v David Kestenbaum>But then, we came across this phone call. And it just seemed important to put it on the air now. It's from Monday, before the explosions around the airport that killed dozens of people.
<v David Kestenbaum>但后来，我们偶然发现了这个通话。觉得现在把它播出来很重要。这是周一的通话，发生在机场周围发生爆炸、造成数十人死亡之前。

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:20.550
<v David Kestenbaum>But it's a snapshot, in a way, from the last hours of this war, as the US leaves Afghanistan, when who would get out and who would not was all still being decided hour by hour. So what we're going to do on the show today is we're going to hear the story of that phone call. And then we're going to play you some other stories about getting out-- about people trying to extricate themselves from seemingly impossible situations.
<v David Kestenbaum>但从某种程度上说，这是这场战争最后时刻的一个缩影，当时美国正在撤离阿富汗，谁能走、谁不能走，一切都在每小时不断变化。所以，我们今天节目要做的是，讲述这个电话背后的故事。然后，我们将播放其他关于“离开”的故事——讲述人们试图从看似不可能的绝境中脱身的故事。

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:44.150
<v David Kestenbaum>So the first guy you heard on the call, the guy in California, is Kirk Johnson. We've had him on the show before. And when things started to fall apart in Afghanistan, he reached out to us, because years ago, he did reconstruction work in Iraq for the US government. And since then, for a long time now, he's been trying to get people in Iraq and Afghanistan who have worked with the US government and who are in danger out of those countries.
<v David Kestenbaum>你刚才在通话中听到的第一个人，那个在加州的人，是柯克·约翰逊。我们以前邀请过他上节目。当阿富汗局势开始恶化时，他联系了我们，因为多年前他曾为美国政府在伊拉克做过重建工作。从那时起，很长一段时间以来，他一直试图帮助那些曾与美国政府合作、目前身处险境的伊拉克和阿富汗人离开这些国家。

00:01:44.150 --> 00:02:00.750
<v David Kestenbaum>He started an organization back in 2007 called The List Project. So these past weeks, Kirk has been on the phone constantly. And on Monday night, with his kids asleep in the other room, he had this series of phone calls which he recorded with the man you heard. His name is Ajmal.
<v David Kestenbaum>他在2007年创办了一个名为“名单计划”（The List Project）的组织。所以过去几周，柯克一直在不停地接打电话。周一晚上，孩子们在另一个房间睡着后，他与你刚才听到的那个人进行了一系列通话，并录了音。那人的名字叫Ajmal。

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:23.750
<v David Kestenbaum>And Kirk is trying to talk him through the crowds, and the confusion, and chaos around the airport in Afghanistan to try to get Ajmal and his family past all the barriers, hopefully onto a plane and out of the country. All of this is happening over the phone from half a world away. Nancy Updike here has known Kirk for many years. She talked to him about what happened. Here's Nancy.
<v David Kestenbaum>柯克试图引导他穿过阿富汗机场周围的人群、困惑和混乱，设法让Ajmal一家越过所有障碍，希望能登上飞机离开这个国家。所有这一切都是通过电话在半个地球之外进行的。南希·厄普代克认识柯克很多年了。她和柯克聊了聊当时的情况。接下来是南希。

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:49.490
<v Nancy Updike>Kirk didn't know Ajmal before Monday night. He never met him. He just heard this was a person who needed help-- one of the many people who needed help, whose names have been piling up in these threads Kirk has been on all week-- group chats with US veterans, active military, current and former diplomats and aid workers, some Afghan citizens of the US and other countries, all on their phones day and night, trying to help Afghans get out.
<v Nancy Updike>柯克在周一晚上之前并不认识Ajmal。他从未见过他。他只是听说这个人需要帮助——这是众多需要帮助的人之一，他们的名字在柯克整周参与的那些群聊中堆积如山——那是与美国退伍军人、现役军人、现任和前任外交官及援助人员，以及一些美国和其他国家的阿富汗公民建立的群组，他们日夜守在电话旁，试图帮助阿富汗人离开。

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:50.390
<v Kirk Johnson>Ajmal, can you hear me?
<v Kirk Johnson>Ajmal，你能听到我说话吗？

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.130
<v Nancy Updike>Monday night, Kirk called Ajmal.
<v Nancy Updike>周一晚上，柯克给Ajmal打了电话。

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.190
<v Ajmal>I can hear you.
<v Ajmal>我能听到。

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:58.310
<v Kirk Johnson>OK, so are you still in front of the Canadian, or not?
<v Kirk Johnson>好的，你现在还在那个加拿大人前面吗？

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:02.450
<v Ajmal>Yeah, in front of the Canadian, in the water.
<v Ajmal>在的，在加拿大人前面，在水里。

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.540
<v Kirk Johnson>You're in the water right now?
<v Kirk Johnson>你现在在水里？

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:06.710
<v Ajmal>Yes.
<v Ajmal>是的。

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:12.320
<v Nancy Updike>The Canadian is a Canadian soldier, and the water is the canal right outside the Kabul airport.
<v Nancy Updike>“加拿大人”指的是一名加拿大士兵，“水”是喀布尔机场外的一条运河。

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:13.520
<v Ajmal>[? This ?] is not [INAUDIBLE], don't know--
<v Ajmal>[？这个？]不是[听不清]，不知道——

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:24.490
<v Nancy Updike>The only thing Ajmal is trying to do here, the whole thing, is to slog his way to within earshot of the one Canadian soldier he can see and make himself heard above the crowd.
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal在这里所做的唯一一件事，就是拼命挪动到他所能看到的那个加拿大士兵的听力范围内，并确保在人群的嘈杂声中能让人听到他的声音。

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:27.310
<v Nancy Updike>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Nancy Updike>[说话声嘈杂]

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:29.660
<v Ajmal>Canada!
<v Ajmal>加拿大！

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:47.330
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal worked for an NGO funded by the United States. And now, he and his family need to leave-- him, his parents, and his brother. They're luckier than most of the people around them, because they have citizenship in another country. He and his family are all Canadian citizens.
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal曾为一个由美国资助的非政府组织工作。现在，他和他的家人——他、他的父母和他的兄弟——需要离开。他们比周围的大多数人幸运，因为他们拥有另一个国家的公民身份。他和他的家人都是加拿大公民。

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:58.010
<v Nancy Updike>And please picture this young Canadian, Ajmal. Kirk sent me a photo of him in the canal. Ajmal is 27 years old. Pretty sure he could pass for 19 without his goatee.
<v Nancy Updike>请想象一下这个年轻的加拿大人Ajmal。柯克发给我一张他在运河里的照片。Ajmal今年27岁。我敢肯定，如果不留山羊胡子，他看起来就像19岁。

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:16.820
<v Nancy Updike>He's slight, big glasses, wearing his backpack in front of him on his chest, like a nervous tourist. He is knee-deep in water. On one side of the canal is a growing crowd of Afghans. On the other are soldiers. And behind the soldiers is the path to get into the airport.
<v Nancy Updike>他身材瘦小，戴着一副大眼镜，像个紧张的游客一样把背包背在胸前。他站在及膝深的水里。运河的一边是越来越多的人群，另一边是士兵。而在士兵身后，就是进入机场的通道。

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:19.610
<v Ajmal>Do-- do you know their name, or no?
<v Ajmal>你——你知道他们的名字吗，或者不知道？

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:24.240
<v Kirk Johnson>The-- no. The soldier's name? No.
<v Kirk Johnson>那个——不。士兵的名字？不知道。

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.560
<v Ajmal>Please wait, OK?
<v Ajmal>请等一下，好吗？

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.350
<v Kirk Johnson>OK, I'm waiting.
<v Kirk Johnson>好的，我在等。

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:38.970
<v Nancy Updike>Exactly how Ajmal came to be standing in a canal is, hours earlier, he'd received an email with instructions for what to do, how to get out. He forwarded the email to Kirk.
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal到底是怎么站到运河里的呢？几个小时前，他收到了一封邮件，里面有关于怎么做、怎么撤离的说明。他把这封邮件转发给了柯克。

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:48.770
<v Kirk Johnson>Here's the email that Ajmal got at 3:01 AM from-- from the Canadian government.
<v Kirk Johnson>这是Ajmal在凌晨3:01收到的来自——来自加拿大政府的邮件。

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:50.660
<v Nancy Updike>Kirk read the key part.
<v Nancy Updike>柯克读了其中的关键部分。

00:04:50.660 --> 00:05:14.890
<v Kirk Johnson>Important instructions-- go to The Baron Hotel immediately-- all caps. Do not wear Western style clothing-- all caps. Be prepared to wait for many hours. Once inside, go to the garden and sit under the Canadian flag and wait for Canadian officials. Bring food and water, if possible.
<v Kirk Johnson>重要指示——立即前往男爵酒店（The Baron Hotel）——全大写。不要穿西式服装——全大写。做好等待数小时的准备。一旦进入，请到花园坐在加拿大国旗下面，等待加拿大官员。如果可能，带上食物和水。

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:35.310
<v Nancy Updike>That's the official process here. Get yourself to a hotel garden, sit down under a flag. Do this immediately somehow. Bring water. Ajmal and his family had gone to the place they thought was The Baron Hotel, but it was the wrong place. No Canadian officials they could see-- seemed to be all British people.
<v Nancy Updike>这就是这里的官方程序。设法赶到酒店花园，坐在国旗下面。无论如何立即照做。带上水。Ajmal一家去了他们以为是男爵酒店的地方，但地点错了。他们看不到任何加拿大官员——看起来全是英国人。

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:53.910
<v Nancy Updike>So Kirk drew a map, looking at Google Maps, with the route Ajmal, his parents, and his brother needed to take, through more crowds, to get to the right place. The canal, believe it or not, was the right place-- or as close as they were able to get. The canal is next to The Baron Hotel.
<v Nancy Updike>于是柯克看着谷歌地图画了一张路线图，指引Ajmal、他的父母和兄弟需要穿过更多的人群到达正确的地方。信不信由你，运河就是那个正确的地方——或者说是他们能到达的最接近的地方。运河就在男爵酒店旁边。

00:05:53.910 --> 00:06:02.070
<v Nancy Updike>And there was a Canadian soldier there. They could see him. He had a little Canadian flag on his uniform. But he kept stepping away.
<v Nancy Updike>那里有一个加拿大士兵。他们能看到他。他的制服上有一个小小的加拿大国旗。但他一直在走开。

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:03.000
<v Kirk Johnson>OK, is he back?
<v Kirk Johnson>好的，他回来了吗？

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.060
<v Ajmal>Yeah, they are talking with the [INAUDIBLE].
<v Ajmal>是的，他们正在和[听不清]谈话。

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.270
<v Kirk Johnson>He's talking to someone else right now?
<v Kirk Johnson>他现在正在和别人说话吗？

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:07.890
<v Ajmal>He's talking with someone else. I'm waiting for him.
<v Ajmal>他正在和别人说话。我在等他。

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:13.030
<v Kirk Johnson>OK. Jesus Christ.
<v Kirk Johnson>天哪。

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:35.060
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal is trying to get the soldier's attention, because, as I keep saying, Ajmal is a citizen of Canada. He is not a supplicant. He has the right papers. But Ajmal, who comes across as a cautious, rule-following sort of person, with his face mask pulled neatly under his chin in case he's ever inside again, that young man is stuck in a canal.
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal正试图引起那个士兵的注意，因为我一直在说，Ajmal是加拿大公民。他不是乞求者。他有合法的证件。但是Ajmal，一个看起来谨慎、遵守规则的人，口罩整齐地拉在下巴下面以防万一再次进入室内，那个年轻人被困在运河里了。

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:36.020
<v Kirk Johnson>I'll be patient. Don't--
<v Kirk Johnson>我会耐心的。别——

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:42.340
<v Ajmal>Excuse me? Hey, Canada? Canada?
<v Ajmal>打扰一下？嘿，加拿大？加拿大？

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:43.670
<v Kirk Johnson>Oh my god.
<v Kirk Johnson>天哪。

00:06:43.670 --> 00:07:02.520
<v Nancy Updike>Other people in this same situation might have no trouble bellowing to get someone's attention. Soft-spoken Ajmal is saying, excuse me, excuse me, Canada? The soldier is talking to other people or walking even further away. Ajmal asks Kirk what words he should be calling out.
<v Nancy Updike>处境相同的人可能毫无顾忌地大喊以引起别人的注意。说话轻声细语的Ajmal一直在说：打扰一下，打扰一下，加拿大？士兵在和别人说话，或者走得更远了。Ajmal问柯克他应该喊什么词。

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:14.590
<v Ajmal>What's a good word to call them? Because they're a bit far.
<v Ajmal>有什么好的词可以喊他们吗？因为他们离得有点远。

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:19.340
<v Kirk Johnson>When you You get an opportunity, Ajmal, OK-- can you hear me?
<v Kirk Johnson>当你有机会的时候，Ajmal，好的——你能听到我说话吗？

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:20.590
<v Ajmal>Then I call you?
<v Ajmal>然后我再喊你？

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:34.250
<v Kirk Johnson>No, I'm going to stay on the line with you. When he finishes with this guy, you need to say that there is a friend of a Canadian minister on the phone. Can you--
<v Kirk Johnson>不，我会一直和你保持通话。当他结束和那个人的谈话时，你需要说电话里有一位加拿大部长的朋友。你能——

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:35.390
<v Ajmal>OK, OK, OK.
<v Ajmal>好的，好的，好的。

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:38.950
<v Kirk Johnson>OK? A friend of a Canadian minister on the phone.
<v Kirk Johnson>好的吗？电话里有一位加拿大部长的朋友。

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:57.220
<v Nancy Updike>It's true. Kirk, through his wife, is friends with a Canadian member of parliament, a minister, who, at that point, was fast asleep and had no idea this was happening. Kirk is trying to think of anything he can tell Ajmal to say to get the soldier's attention. Namedropping? Yes, sure, definitely.
<v Nancy Updike>这是真的。柯克通过他的妻子，确实认识一位加拿大国会议员，一位部长。那时那个人正在熟睡，根本不知道发生了什么。柯克试图想出任何他能告诉Ajmal的话，以引起士兵的注意。套近乎？是的，当然，肯定。

00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:04.420
<v Ajmal>[? Yes, ?] [? just-- ?] just a minute.
<v Ajmal>[？是的，？][？只要——？]等一分钟。

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:08.790
<v Kirk Johnson>Where is he?
<v Kirk Johnson>他在哪？

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:13.330
<v Ajmal>Uh, he left [INAUDIBLE]. I'm waiting for him.
<v Ajmal>呃，他离开了[听不清]。我在等他。

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:20.100
<v Kirk Johnson>OK, I don't want you to just say Canada. I want you to say, I'm a Canadian citizen.
<v Kirk Johnson>好的，我不希望你只喊加拿大。我要你喊，我是加拿大公民。

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.090
<v Ajmal>OK, OK.
<v Ajmal>好的，好的。

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:35.760
<v Kirk Johnson>He doesn't know me. He's putting trust in me. I don't know him. I've already pushed him to fight through the crowd and jump into the canal.
<v Kirk Johnson>他不认识我。他信任我。我也不认识他。我已经推着他穿过人群跳进运河里了。

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:37.560
<v Nancy Updike>That was your idea, to get in the canal?
<v Nancy Updike>那是你的主意，让他进运河？

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:37.810
<v Kirk Johnson>Yes.
<v Kirk Johnson>是的。

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:39.110
<v Nancy Updike>Huh.
<v Nancy Updike>嗯。

00:08:39.110 --> 00:09:09.760
<v Kirk Johnson>By the time of that phone call, that was already-- when he got to the back end of that crowd and saw thousands of people, he said, there's-- I can't do this. There's no-- I have my parents here. We can't get to the front. We're just going to have to try another day, or wait for you to work your connections to get the Canadians to come out and find us.
<v Kirk Johnson>在那个通话的时候，这已经是——当他到达人群的后方看到成千上万的人时，他说，我做不到。这里没有——我的父母在这里。我们无法到达前面。我们只能改天再试，或者等你通过人脉让加拿大人出来找我们。

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:28.720
<v Kirk Johnson>And I told him-- this is over text-- but I was like, they're not going to come out for you. You have to push through that crowd. And his sister was on the thread, and a couple other people who had been trying to help him.
<v Kirk Johnson>我告诉他——这是通过短信说的——我说，他们不会出来找你的。你必须推开人群穿过去。他的妹妹也在群里，还有其他几个一直试图帮助他的人。

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:44.800
<v Kirk Johnson>And they were messaging me, saying, go easy on him. He was in the hospital last week with a panic attack. You know, they were basically saying, he's already at the breaking point.
<v Kirk Johnson>他们给我发信息说，对他好点。他上周因为惊恐发作进了医院。你知道，他们基本上是在说，他已经到了崩溃的边缘。

00:09:44.800 --> 00:10:18.060
<v Kirk Johnson>But I knew that, if he couldn't get within earshot of a Canadian soldier, he had-- there was nothing. Not knowing, if he gives up today, whether or not the doors will be completely shut tomorrow. And the other thing that is weighing on me, but that I'm not telling him, is this dread of one rogue Talib opening fire into that crowd, one IED going off in that crowd.
<v Kirk Johnson>但我知道，如果他不能进入加拿大士兵的听力范围内，他就——什么都没有了。不知道如果他今天放弃，明天门是否会完全关闭。还有一件事让我很担心，但我没有告诉他，那就是恐惧会有塔利班分子向人群开火，或者人群中发生爆炸。

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:25.560
<v Nancy Updike>Less than 24 hours after we talked, a bomb was detonated outside the airport. And someone started shooting into the crowd.
<v Nancy Updike>在我们通话不到24小时后，机场外发生了炸弹爆炸。有人开始向人群射击。

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:40.950
<v Kirk Johnson>So everything came down to trying to get him in the right point on Earth where his voice would be loud enough to be heard, and strong enough to be listened to.
<v Kirk Johnson>所以一切归结于试图把他送到地球上正确的位置，让他的声音足够大，能够被听到，足够有力，能够被倾听。

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:43.100
<v Kirk Johnson>You are in the right position.
<v Kirk Johnson>你现在处于正确的位置了。

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:44.460
<v Ajmal>But I think they are leaving.
<v Ajmal>但我认为他们要离开了。

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:48.540
<v Kirk Johnson>What-- what do you-- OK.
<v Kirk Johnson>什么——你——好的。

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:54.090
<v Ajmal>I think they're leaving the spot. Please, please talk to them.
<v Ajmal>我想他们要离开那个位置了。求求你，求求你和他们说话。

00:10:54.090 --> 00:11:02.100
<v Kirk Johnson>You have-- you have to shout, I'm a Canadian citizen. Do it right now, if you see them.
<v Kirk Johnson>你必须——你必须大喊，我是加拿大公民。如果你看到他们，马上做。

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:03.420
<v Ajmal>Canadian!
<v Ajmal>加拿大！

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:13.950
<v Kirk Johnson>Not, Canadian. Say, Canadian citizen. You can't just say Canadian, Ajmal. You have to say Canadian citizen.
<v Kirk Johnson>不是加拿大，说，加拿大公民。你不能只说加拿大，Ajmal。你必须说加拿大公民。

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:14.610
<v Ajmal>I'll call them.
<v Ajmal>我会叫他们的。

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:15.480
<v Kirk Johnson>OK.
<v Kirk Johnson>好的。

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.220
<v Ajmal>Canadian citizen!
<v Ajmal>加拿大公民！

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:20.280
<v Kirk Johnson>Keep saying that.
<v Kirk Johnson>继续喊那个。

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:21.820
<v Ajmal>Canadian citizen!
<v Ajmal>加拿大公民！

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:23.120
<v Kirk Johnson>[BLEEP]
<v Kirk Johnson>[消音]

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:30.470
<v Nancy Updike>If you have sharp ears, you can hear Kirk swearing under his breath all during this call, basically cursing his way through the stress.
<v Nancy Updike>如果你耳朵尖的话，可以听到柯克在整个通话过程中一直在低声咒骂，基本上是通过咒骂来缓解压力。

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:34.110
<v Kirk Johnson>What's happening?
<v Kirk Johnson>发生了什么？

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:37.040
<v Ajmal>I think the [INAUDIBLE] is increasing. I don't know what's happening.
<v Ajmal>我想[听不清]在增加。我不知道发生了什么。

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:40.220
<v Nancy Updike>He's saying the crowd is increasing. It's increasing.
<v Nancy Updike>他说人群在增加。在增加。

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:42.110
<v Ajmal>The crowds are increasing.
<v Ajmal>人群在增加。

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:42.680
<v Kirk Johnson>I know.
<v Kirk Johnson>我知道。

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:44.120
<v Ajmal>--crowd is increasing [INAUDIBLE]--
<v Ajmal>——人群在增加[听不清]——

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:49.270
<v Kirk Johnson>I know, but you are in the right-- you are in the right position. You can't give up right now. You--
<v Kirk Johnson>我知道，但你在正确的位置——你就在正确的位置。你现在不能放弃。你——

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:56.270
<v Ajmal>OK, so I am inside, so-- but my family is down. They are-- they are back.
<v Ajmal>好的，我现在在里面，所以——但我的家人在下面。他们——他们在后面。

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:59.660
<v Kirk Johnson>OK. [GROANS] How far away are they?
<v Kirk Johnson>好的。[呻吟声]他们离你有多远？

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:24.110
<v Nancy Updike>Kirk is groaning, because he didn't realize Ajmal was separated from his family, his parents, and his brother at this point. Even if they're not that far away right now, while Ajmal and Kirk are focused on connecting with this soldier, Ajmal's family could get pushed out of sight as the crowd keeps swelling and pushing forward. A bad scenario would be Ajmal ends up getting out, but his family stays lost in the throng.
<v Nancy Updike>柯克在呻吟，因为他没意识到Ajmal此时已经与家人、父母和兄弟分开了。即使他们现在离得不远，当Ajmal和柯克专注于与那个士兵联系时，Ajmal的家人可能会随着人群不断涌动和推进而消失在视线之外。最坏的情况是Ajmal成功出去了，但他的家人却迷失在人群中。

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:48.440
<v Nancy Updike>On these group threads, these chats Kirk is following, they are full of stories of family members getting separated. Ajmal hadn't wanted to bring his parents and his brother into this gross canal unless it was clear they were going to get out. He said the bottom of the canal had these slippery stones, and he worried one of his parents would fall and injure themselves. Now there's no time for Ajmal to break off and go get them. He has to keep trying to reach this soldier.
<v Nancy Updike>在这些群聊中，柯克关注的那些聊天记录里，全是家人失散的故事。Ajmal不想带父母和兄弟进这条恶心的运河，除非能确保他们能离开。他说运河底部有湿滑的石头，他担心父母中的一人会摔倒受伤。现在Ajmal没时间分身去接他们了。他必须继续努力接触那个士兵。

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:56.330
<v Kirk Johnson>You can't lose that spot. You have that-- you had that soldier right in front of you. He's going to come back.
<v Kirk Johnson>你不能失去那个位置。你有那个——你刚才就在那个士兵面前。他会回来的。

00:12:56.330 --> 00:13:08.310
<v Ajmal>So here-- he's so far. I think it's impossible. Is there any other way?
<v Ajmal>所以这里——他太远了。我觉得这不可能。还有其他办法吗？

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:13.470
<v Kirk Johnson>You have to stay in that spot and wait for him to come back. The fact that you just sent me that picture--
<v Kirk Johnson>你必须待在那个位置，等他回来。你刚才发给我的那张照片，事实就是——

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:15.000
<v Ajmal>I'll wait-- yeah, I'm waiting.
<v Ajmal>我会等的——是的，我在等。

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:15.260
<v Kirk Johnson>OK.
<v Kirk Johnson>好的。

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:16.460
<v Ajmal>So I will call you back, OK?
<v Ajmal>所以我再给你打电话，好吗？

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:20.800
<v Kirk Johnson>Call me back as soon as he gets close, OK?
<v Kirk Johnson>他一靠近就再给我打电话，好吗？

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:23.650
<v Ajmal>OK, OK, OK.
<v Ajmal>好的，好的，好的。

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:45.820
<v Nancy Updike>Just in case anyone is finding their ire at this point focused on Canada, I want to state the obvious and say that, in the big picture, this was our show, the United States-- our plans, our timeline. Those were dictating the overall scene at the airport. Ajmal and Kirk got back on the phone. They'd been at it for almost six hours.
<v Nancy Updike>以防有人此时对加拿大心生愤恨，我想陈述一个显而易见的事实：从大局来看，这是我们美国人的节目——我们的计划，我们的时间表。这些主导了机场的整体局面。Ajmal和柯克重新接通了电话。他们已经这样持续了近六个小时。

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:51.630
<v Kirk Johnson>Do not let him go away without saying, you're a Canadian citizen, and a minister is on the phone.
<v Kirk Johnson>不要让他走开，一定要说，你是加拿大公民，而且一位部长在电话里。

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:55.110
<v Kirk Johnson>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Kirk Johnson>[说话声嘈杂]

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:57.810
<v Nancy Updike>A long minute goes by where Kirk can't tell what's happening.
<v Nancy Updike>漫长的一分钟过去了，柯克看不出发生了什么。

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:02.290
<v Nancy Updike>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Nancy Updike>[说话声嘈杂]

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:05.930
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal keeps asking him, please wait, please wait.
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal一直请求他，请等一下，请等一下。

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:06.940
<v Ajmal>Please wait here.
<v Ajmal>请在这里等一下。

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:18.220
<v Kirk Johnson>Don't-- don't worry about me. Just do not let him leave without saying what I told you.
<v Kirk Johnson>别——别担心我。只要在他走之前说出我告诉你的那些话就行。

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:24.640
<v Nancy Updike>Another half minute. Ajmal is fighting off despair. The soldier isn't responding to him.
<v Nancy Updike>又过了半分钟。Ajmal正在努力抵御绝望。士兵没有回应他。

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:28.960
<v Ajmal>So he is not responding, he just told me.
<v Ajmal>所以他没有回应，他刚才告诉我的。

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:32.470
<v Kirk Johnson>What did you say?
<v Kirk Johnson>你说什么？

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:37.660
<v Ajmal>He is so [INAUDIBLE].
<v Ajmal>他太[听不清]了。

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:38.860
<v Kirk Johnson>Are you still close to him?
<v Kirk Johnson>你还在他附近吗？

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:49.450
<v Kirk Johnson>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Kirk Johnson>[说话声嘈杂]

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:51.550
<v Nancy Updike>Another agonizing minute and a half--
<v Nancy Updike>又过了痛苦的一分半钟——

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:53.650
<v Nancy Updike>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Nancy Updike>[说话声嘈杂]

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:54.970
<v Nancy Updike>--and then suddenly--
<v Nancy Updike>——然后突然——

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:59.250
<v Kirk Johnson>Ajmal, can you hear me?
<v Kirk Johnson>Ajmal，你能听到我说话吗？

00:14:59.250 --> 00:14:59.950
<v Man>Hello?
<v Man>喂？

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:01.630
<v Kirk Johnson>Hello, who is this?
<v Kirk Johnson>喂，你是谁？

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:03.230
<v Man>Hello?
<v Man>喂？

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:04.690
<v Kirk Johnson>Yes, who am I talking to?
<v Kirk Johnson>是的，我在和谁说话？

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:05.400
<v Man>Who's this?
<v Man>你是谁？

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:08.020
<v Kirk Johnson>This is Kirk Johnson. I've served with USAID in Iraq--
<v Kirk Johnson>我是柯克·约翰逊。我曾在伊拉克服役于美国国际开发署——

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:08.920
<v Man>[INAUDIBLE]
<v Man>[听不清]

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:12.110
<v Kirk Johnson>--sorry, can you hear me?
<v Kirk Johnson>——抱歉，你能听到我说话吗？

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:12.760
<v Man>Yes, I can hear you.
<v Man>是的，我能听到。

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:26.700
<v Kirk Johnson>OK, you have four Canadian citizens there. I'm calling on behalf of Mélanie Joly, who is a minister of economic development for Canada. You have four Canadian citizens. They were asked to go in there right now. And they've been pushed back several times so far.
<v Kirk Johnson>好的，你有四名加拿大公民在那里。我是代表加拿大经济发展部部长梅拉妮·乔利（Mélanie Joly）打电话的。你有四名加拿大公民。他们被要求立即进入那里。而且到目前为止他们已经被推回去好几次了。

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:32.110
<v Man>Yeah, we're going to process them. In the meantime, there's a lot of people here. We will process them, OK?
<v Man>是的，我们会处理他们的。同时，这里有很多人。我们会处理他们的，好吗？

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:40.860
<v Kirk Johnson>I understand, but I just need your assurance that they're going to get through, because one of the group is in poor health. And I know that Mélanie Joly is--
<v Kirk Johnson>我明白，但我只需要你的保证他们能进去，因为其中一人健康状况不佳。而且我知道梅拉妮·乔利正在——

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:41.170
<v Man>Yeah, yeah, we will--
<v Man>是的，是的，我们会——

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:43.630
<v Kirk Johnson>--she's-- the minister is wanting-- she wants it--
<v Kirk Johnson>——她——部长想要——她想要——

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:46.240
<v Man>They will get through. They just--
<v Man>他们会进去的。他们只是——

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:48.190
<v Kirk Johnson>I need to be able to tell the minister that they're through.
<v Kirk Johnson>我需要能够告诉部长他们已经进去了。

00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:54.160
<v Man>Yeah, I understand what you're saying, sir. I'm going to look at his paperwork, and they will be able to get through, OK?
<v Man>是的，我明白你的意思，先生。我会看一下他的文件，他们就能进去的，好吗？

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:56.920
<v Kirk Johnson>OK, I'm going to stay on the line. Thank you for doing that, sir. I appreciate it.
<v Kirk Johnson>好的，我会保持通话。谢谢你这样做，先生。我很感激。

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:58.000
<v Man>--need to be patient. It's--
<v Man>——需要耐心。这是——

00:15:58.000 --> 00:15:59.770
<v Kirk Johnson>I know-- I know you got your hands full.
<v Kirk Johnson>我知道——我知道你手头有很多工作。

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:01.480
<v Man>--pretty hectic here, so we have to be patient, OK?
<v Man>——这里很忙乱，所以我们必须耐心，好吗？

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:02.710
<v Kirk Johnson>I know. I'll tell them to say--
<v Kirk Johnson>我知道。我会告诉他们要说——

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:03.310
<v Man>Tell them to be patient.
<v Man>告诉他们要耐心。

00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:07.100
<v Kirk Johnson>I'm grateful to you for your help, sir. Thank you.
<v Kirk Johnson>我很感激你的帮助，先生。谢谢。

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:10.390
<v Man>OK. I'll give him the phone back.
<v Man>好的。我会把电话还给他。

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:11.890
<v Kirk Johnson>OK, I'm going to stay on the line.
<v Kirk Johnson>好的，我会保持通话。

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:14.500
<v Man>[INAUDIBLE]
<v Man>[听不清]

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:27.010
<v Nancy Updike>It's not clear if the Canadian minister gambit carried any sway with this soldier. He mostly sounds like he wanted to get off the phone and get back to work. But Ajmal finally had his attention.
<v Nancy Updike>尚不清楚加拿大部长的计策是否对这个士兵产生了影响。他听起来更像是想挂断电话回去工作。但Ajmal终于引起了他的注意。

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:53.170
<v Nancy Updike>And what a frightening degree of perseverance it took Ajmal to get to this point, where he could just hand someone his papers. And even perseverance most likely wouldn't have been enough without luck, without connections to these groups Kirk and others are part of, or to someone else with power. How many people in the crowd around Ajmal had perseverance, even courage, but none of the luck?
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal为了到达这一步需要多么令人恐惧的毅力，他才能把证件交给某人。即便如此，如果没有运气，没有与柯克等人所属的这些群组的联系，或者没有与某个有权势的人的联系，毅力很可能是不够的。在Ajmal周围的人群中，有多少人有毅力，甚至勇气，却没有任何运气？

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:54.090
<v Kirk Johnson>OK, Ajmal--
<v Kirk Johnson>好的，Ajmal——

00:16:54.090 --> 00:16:54.940
<v Ajmal>Hello?
<v Ajmal>喂？

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:56.530
<v Kirk Johnson>Yeah, can you hear me?
<v Kirk Johnson>是的，你能听到我说话吗？

00:16:56.530 --> 00:16:56.890
<v Ajmal>Yes.
<v Ajmal>是的。

00:16:56.890 --> 00:17:02.230
<v Kirk Johnson>OK, get your family close to you. OK? I don't care--
<v Kirk Johnson>好的，让你的家人靠近你。好的吗？我不在乎——

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:03.260
<v Ajmal>Tell my family to come?
<v Ajmal>告诉我的家人过来？

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:07.960
<v Kirk Johnson>Tell-- tell them to come. You may have to wait a while, but do not-- do not be separate from them now.
<v Kirk Johnson>告诉——告诉他们过来。你可能需要等一会儿，但不要——不要现在和他们分开。

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:08.410
<v Ajmal>Yes.
<v Ajmal>是的。

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:10.080
<v Kirk Johnson>Do not move where you are, OK?
<v Kirk Johnson>不要离开你所在的地方，好吗？

00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:11.420
<v Ajmal>OK, thank you, sir.
<v Ajmal>好的，谢谢你，先生。

00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:11.760
<v Kirk Johnson>OK.
<v Kirk Johnson>好的。

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:15.200
<v Ajmal>OK, OK.
<v Ajmal>好的，好的。

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:32.340
<v Nancy Updike>Kirk and I did this interview a day and a half after this call with Ajmal ended. And Kirk was still waiting to hear if Ajmal and his family made it out. Then, finally, he got a text from him. It came through as Kirk and I were finishing up our interview.
<v Nancy Updike>在Ajmal的那个电话结束后一天半，我和柯克进行了这次采访。柯克仍在等待Ajmal和他的家人是否成功离开的消息。然后，他终于收到了一条短信。当我和柯克结束采访时，短信传了过来。

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:43.920
<v Kirk Johnson>He texted me-- hang on-- oh my god.
<v Kirk Johnson>他给我发了短信——等等——噢天哪。

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:47.300
<v Nancy Updike>What?
<v Nancy Updike>什么？

00:17:47.300 --> 00:17:50.660
<v Kirk Johnson>They just got on a plane. They're--
<v Kirk Johnson>他们刚刚登上了一架飞机。他们——

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:52.090
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal and his family, they're on a plane?
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal和他的家人，他们在飞机上？

00:17:52.090 --> 00:18:00.970
<v Kirk Johnson>Yes.
<v Kirk Johnson>是的。

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:16.360
<v Nancy Updike>There's one part of Ajmal's story that took Kirk's breath away, even in the retelling. It was the way Ajmal ended up finding his brother after he'd made contact with the soldier, because Ajmal's family, as Kirk had feared, had gotten lost in the crowd.
<v Nancy Updike>Ajmal故事里有一个部分让柯克屏住呼吸，即使在复述时也是如此。那就是Ajmal在联系到那个士兵后找到他兄弟的方式，因为正如柯克所担心的，Ajmal的家人已经在人群中走散了。

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:59.060
<v Kirk Johnson>You don't have to have brothers to feel this, but my brothers are my best friends. And the way that I subsequently found out he was able to-- to get them was that he just called from the canal into this crowd, asking his fellow Afghans to start calling his brother's name. And they all started shouting it out. And that's how they found his-- his brother and parents. And so they then got into the canal from there and joined him.
<v Kirk Johnson>你不需要有兄弟也能感受到这一点，但我的兄弟是我最好的朋友。后来我发现他能够——找到他们的方法是，他就在运河里对着人群大喊，请求他的阿富汗同胞开始呼喊他兄弟的名字。他们都开始大声喊叫。那就是他们找到他——他的兄弟和父母的方式。于是他们从那里进了运河并和他汇合了。

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:36.020
<v Kirk Johnson>You know, like, I want to feel-- I want to feel like, proud about this, and feel like this is a good story. But it's just-- there's so much more suffering that has happened, and people that aren't getting through. I don't want people who hear this to think that-- you know, people who are sort of discomfited by what they're hearing about this, who may not really be close to the situation or understand what's going on, to be like, oh, hey, I heard it's bad, but hey, all right, good. Now, this guy-- this has a happy ending.
<v Kirk Johnson>你知道，就像，我想感到——感到骄傲，觉得这是一个好故事。但这只是——发生了太多太多的苦难，还有很多人没能过去。我不希望听到这个故事的人认为——你知道，那些对他们所听到的事情感到不安的人，那些可能并不了解情况或不明白发生了什么的人，会觉得，哦，嘿，我听说情况很糟糕，但嘿，好吧，太好了。现在，这个人——这有一个圆满的结局。

00:19:36.020 --> 00:20:09.050
<v Kirk Johnson>This has a happy ending for this guy and his family, and for a small percentage of the lucky few that are getting through. I-- I fear that we're-- you know, we're not going to really see the full toll of this when this is all over. And we're-- we're just about at the end of this whole disaster, and at the start of something much darker, I fear.
<v Kirk Johnson>这对他和他的家人，以及少数幸运通过的幸运儿来说，是一个圆满的结局。我——我担心我们——你知道，当这一切结束时，我们真的无法看到全部的代价。而且我们——我们正处于整个灾难的尾声，但我担心，我们正处于一个更加黑暗的开端。

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:23.940
<v Nancy Updike>I fear that, too.
<v Nancy Updike>我也担心这一点。

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:44.740
<v David Kestenbaum>Nancy Updike is one of the producers of our show. Coming up, some of our favorite stories we've run about getting out, including a choose your own adventure story about getting out of a relationship. Yeah, choose your own adventure on the radio, somehow. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v David Kestenbaum>南希·厄普代克是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来，我们将分享一些关于“离开”的我们最喜欢的故事，包括一个关于从一段关系中脱身的“选择你自己的冒险”故事。是的，某种程度上，广播里的“选择你自己的冒险”。稍后节目继续，来自芝加哥公共广播电台。

00:20:44.740 --> 00:20:55.480
<v David Kestenbaum>It's This American Life. I'm David Kestenbaum, in for Ira Glass. This week on our show, getting out-- stories of people making it out of seemingly impossible situations, or trying to, anyway.
<v David Kestenbaum>这是《美国生活》。我是大卫·克斯滕鲍姆，代班艾拉·格拉斯。本周我们的节目主题是“离开”——讲述人们从看似不可能的绝境中脱身的故事，或者至少是在尝试脱身的故事。

00:20:55.480 --> 00:21:13.360
<v David Kestenbaum>Act two-- You Can Check Out Any Time You Like, But You Can Never Leave. So one of the things about trying to get out of an intense situation is that it can feel like every little decision you make might take you down the wrong path. Back when she was a teenager, one of our co-workers, Elna Baker, found herself in that position.
<v David Kestenbaum>第二幕——“你可以随时结账，但你永远无法离开”。关于试图从激烈的情况下脱身的一件事是，你可能会觉得你做的每一个小决定都可能让你走上错误的道路。回到她十几岁的时候，我们的同事埃尔娜·贝克（Elna Baker）就处于那个境地。

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:29.020
<v David Kestenbaum>She was trapped in a hotel room with her siblings, held there by a stranger. What happened was, their family was all staying at this Marriott. But they're such a big family that the parents and the oldest kid were in one room. Elna and the three other kids were next door in their own room.
<v David Kestenbaum>她被困在酒店房间里，和她的兄弟姐妹在一起，被一个陌生人扣押在那里。发生的事情是，他们全家都住在万豪酒店。但他们是一个大家庭，所以父母和最大的孩子住在一个房间。埃尔娜和另外三个孩子住在隔壁的房间里。

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:41.680
<v David Kestenbaum>There was Elna, who was 14 at the time, Julia, who was 10, Briton, who was eight, and Jill, who was five. Elna was sharing a bed with her. Elna, who was in eighth grade at the time, was more or less in charge.
<v David Kestenbaum>当时14岁的埃尔娜，10岁的朱莉娅，8岁的布里顿和5岁的吉尔。埃尔娜和她同睡一张床。当时读八年级的埃尔娜或多或少负责照顾他们。

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:46.750
<v David Kestenbaum>And then one night, they're all asleep. It's the middle of the night. She told this all to Ira.
<v David Kestenbaum>然后有一天晚上，他们都睡着了。那是深夜。她把这一切告诉了艾拉。

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:58.960
<v Elna Baker>My memory is that-- it's like-- I saw like, a sliver of light, which is what woke me up, and the sound of the door closing. And there was the feeling that there was someone standing at the foot of the bed.
<v Elna Baker>我的记忆是——那就像——我看到了一线光，那是把我叫醒的东西，还有门关上的声音。有一种感觉，床脚站着一个人。

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:00.190
<v Ira Glass>Mhm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:05.780
<v Elna Baker>So I reached over, and I turned the lamp on. And there was this woman.
<v Elna Baker>所以我伸手打开了灯。那里站着一个女人。

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:06.250
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:22:06.250 --> 00:22:20.070
<v Elna Baker>And she looked startled to see us. And the first thing she said was, you're just children. I didn't expect you to be children.
<v Elna Baker>她看到我们时显得很惊讶。她说的第一句话是，你们只是孩子。我没想到你们是孩子。

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:21.480
<v Ira Glass>So that's pretty creepy.
<v Ira Glass>那真是令人毛骨悚然。

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:22.150
<v Elna Baker>Right.
<v Elna Baker>对。

00:22:22.150 --> 00:22:24.930
<v Ira Glass>What did you make of her saying that?
<v Ira Glass>你对她说那句话怎么看？

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:32.680
<v Elna Baker>I instantly felt like this is an unsafe situation.
<v Elna Baker>我立刻感觉到这是一个不安全的情况。

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:35.850
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Describe her.
<v Ira Glass>是的。描述一下她。

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:40.120
<v Elna Baker>I remember she was my mom's age. So she was probably in her 40s.
<v Elna Baker>我记得她和我妈妈年纪差不多。所以她可能40多岁。

00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:40.740
<v Ira Glass>Mhm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:50.970
<v Elna Baker>And brown hair, white, kind of like, frumpy-looking, like somebody you'd sit next to on the bus or something.
<v Elna Baker>棕色头发，白人，看起来有点邋遢，就像你坐在公共汽车旁边的人一样。

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:53.400
<v Ira Glass>Did she seem menacing?
<v Ira Glass>她看起来有威胁性吗？

00:22:53.400 --> 00:22:59.430
<v Elna Baker>She seemed really distressed. She was crying, too.
<v Elna Baker>她看起来真的很痛苦。她也在哭。

00:22:59.430 --> 00:23:00.240
<v Ira Glass>She was crying?
<v Ira Glass>她在哭？

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:03.880
<v Elna Baker>She was crying, yeah. She cried the whole time she was there.
<v Elna Baker>她在哭，是的。她在那里的整个过程中一直在哭。

00:23:03.880 --> 00:23:08.340
<v Ira Glass>She's crying, and she said, you're children. I didn't expect you to be children.
<v Ira Glass>她在哭，她说，你们是孩子。我没想到你们是孩子。

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:10.350
<v Elna Baker>Yeah.
<v Elna Baker>对。

00:23:10.350 --> 00:23:12.630
<v Ira Glass>And do the other kids get up?
<v Ira Glass>其他孩子醒了吗？

00:23:12.630 --> 00:23:23.730
<v Elna Baker>Everyone's wide awake. I distinctly remember my little brothers' and sisters' faces, because their eyes were just huge. But no one said anything.
<v Elna Baker>所有人都完全清醒了。我清楚地记得我弟弟妹妹们的脸，因为他们的眼睛睁得很大。但没有人说话。

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:26.730
<v Ira Glass>And are they sitting up, too, or are they just laying flat still?
<v Ira Glass>他们也坐起来了吗，还是仍然平躺着？

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:37.180
<v Elna Baker>They're still laying flat. Nobody moved.
<v Elna Baker>他们仍然平躺着。没人动。

00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:38.740
<v Ira Glass>So what happens next?
<v Ira Glass>那么接下来发生了什么？

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:52.870
<v Elna Baker>She's talking, right? This isn't how it was supposed to be. And she's also crying. And she's talking to herself. I remember it being very hard to follow what she was saying.
<v Elna Baker>她在说话，对吧？事情本不该是这样的。而且她也在哭。她在自言自语。我记得很难听懂她在说什么。

00:23:52.870 --> 00:23:53.470
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:23:53.470 --> 00:24:10.170
<v Elna Baker>And eventually, I said, I'm sorry, I think you have the wrong room. And she started to cry. And she said, I know, I know. I do this, I do this. I don't know why I do this.
<v Elna Baker>最后，我说，对不起，我想你走错房间了。她开始哭。她说，我知道，我知道。我做了这个，我做了这个。我不知道为什么我这么做。

00:24:10.170 --> 00:24:19.200
<v Elna Baker>And then she's pacing back and forth, and kind of crying. And she sits down on the bed, at the foot of the bed.
<v Elna Baker>然后她在来回走动，一边哭。她在床脚坐了下来。

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:22.790
<v Ira Glass>Did she seem crazy, or did she seem upset?
<v Ira Glass>她看起来很疯狂，还是看起来很难过？

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:30.640
<v Elna Baker>I mean, it's interesting. I was-- I think I-- I was too young and unfamiliar to identify what a crazy person seems like.
<v Elna Baker>意思是，这很有趣。我当时——我想我——我太年轻，不熟悉什么是疯狂。

00:24:30.640 --> 00:24:31.220
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:37.400
<v Elna Baker>But what I could identify in that moment was that this person was unpredictable.
<v Elna Baker>但我当时能识别出的是，这个人是不可预测的。

00:24:37.400 --> 00:24:40.410
<v Ira Glass>So you guys were like, oh, I've got to be really careful how I do this.
<v Ira Glass>所以你们像，哦，我必须非常小心我怎么做。

00:24:40.410 --> 00:24:52.300
<v Elna Baker>I remember feeling that pressure, and in that moment, very, very, very aware of my brothers and sisters, because they were so little. And--
<v Elna Baker>我记得那种压力，那一刻，非常非常清醒地意识到我的弟弟妹妹们，因为他们太小了。而且——

00:24:52.300 --> 00:24:52.660
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:24:52.660 --> 00:25:17.790
<v Elna Baker>--that something bad could happen to them. And that the way that it wasn't going to happen to them was me. There were no adults there. I think it was the first situation I was ever in where there was-- you couldn't defer to someone else to keep you safe or tell you what to do. And what do I do?
<v Elna Baker>——坏事可能会发生在他们身上。而能阻止坏事发生在他们身上的就是我。那里没有成年人。我认为这是我第一次遇到这种情况，没有——你不能指望别人来保护你或告诉你该怎么做。我该怎么办？

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:45.570
<v Elna Baker>And it had that quality of like-- like, time slows down. And you're kind of-- you're there, but you're also watching. And so then, I did the math in my head. And I was like, OK, I have a couple options. They are cry for help, scream. But I don't think anyone will hear.
<v Elna Baker>它有一种感觉，像——时间变慢了。你有一种——你身处其中，但也像在看戏。于是，我在脑子里计算了一下。我想，好的，我有几个选择。大声求救，尖叫。但我不认为有人会听到。

00:25:45.570 --> 00:25:48.240
<v Ira Glass>Mhm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:25:48.240 --> 00:25:51.360
<v Elna Baker>I remember looking at how far away the phone was from me.
<v Elna Baker>我记得看了一眼电话离我有多远。

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:52.140
<v Ira Glass>Mhm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:25:52.140 --> 00:26:03.410
<v Elna Baker>And I realized I can reach and grab the phone, and I could call for help. Or even like, if I could knock on the door where my sister was--
<v Elna Baker>我意识到我可以伸手去拿电话，我可以求救。或者甚至，如果我能敲隔壁我姐姐的门——

00:26:03.410 --> 00:26:05.870
<v Ira Glass>Sister in the connecting room next door.
<v Ira Glass>在隔壁连通房的姐姐。

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:10.400
<v Elna Baker>I could at least-- they would know something was happening in here. And they could get to us in time.
<v Elna Baker>我至少——她们会知道这里发生了什么。她们可以及时过来。

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:11.510
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:38.100
<v Elna Baker>But all of that felt like an escalation, like I would provoke her. And so what can I do to not provoke her and kind of play up the fact that we're children? And what I felt I needed to do was I needed to calm her down and become her friend. And if I could become her friend, she would leave, and she wouldn't hurt us.
<v Elna Baker>但这感觉像升级，好像我会激怒她。所以我能做什么不激怒她，并利用我们是孩子的事实？我感觉我需要做的是，我需要让她平静下来，成为她的朋友。如果我能成为她的朋友，她就会离开，她就不会伤害我们。

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:40.140
<v Ira Glass>Like, get her to see you as a person?
<v Ira Glass>像，让她把你视为一个人？

00:26:40.140 --> 00:27:10.020
<v Elna Baker>Just make her think I was on her side. Because then-- it didn't feel like she didn't see us as people. It just felt like-- it felt dangerous, and I didn't understand why. And I felt like the key to being safe was slowing everything down.
<v Elna Baker>只是让她认为我和她是同一边的。因为那时——感觉并不是她没把我们看作人。只是感觉——感觉很危险，我不明白为什么。我感觉安全的关键是让一切慢下来。

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:15.530
<v Elna Baker>And I remember her saying, I'm really sorry. I'm sorry. I'm sorry I'm here. I'm sorry I'm doing this. I'm sorry.
<v Elna Baker>我记得她说，我真的很抱歉。对不起。对不起我在这里。对不起我做了这个。对不起。

00:27:15.530 --> 00:27:15.810
<v Ira Glass>Mhm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:27:15.810 --> 00:27:32.420
<v Elna Baker>And I don't remember this, but my brother remembers that what she said was, I'm sorry, girls. I'm sorry, girls. And that his whole memory of this night was just being like, I'm a boy. [LAUGHS] He was so mad.
<v Elna Baker>我不记得这个了，但我弟弟记得她所说的是，对不起，女孩们。对不起，女孩们。而他整晚的记忆就是像，我是个男孩。[笑]他太生气了。

00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:42.970
<v Elna Baker>And so I ask her-- I said, are you OK? Is everything OK? And that's-- she kept, you know, going on about how her night was so hard.
<v Elna Baker>所以我问她——我说，你还好吗？一切都还好吗？那就是——她继续，你知道，说着她的夜晚有多么艰难。

00:27:42.970 --> 00:27:46.890
<v Elna Baker>And I was like, I'm so sorry. That sounds really, really difficult.
<v Elna Baker>我说，我非常抱歉。听起来真的很，真的很困难。

00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:48.660
<v Ira Glass>And this is you trying to befriend her.
<v Ira Glass>这就是你试图和她交朋友。

00:27:48.660 --> 00:28:00.930
<v Elna Baker>Yeah, this is me trying to befriend her. But it's weird, I feel like-- I was hyper alert to like, if I can understand what's going on with her, I can help solve it. But I couldn't understand, because it didn't make any sense.
<v Elna Baker>对，这就是我试图和她交朋友。但这很奇怪，我觉得——我处于高度戒备状态，如果我能理解她发生了什么，我就能帮忙解决。但我无法理解，因为这完全没有意义。

00:28:00.930 --> 00:28:02.820
<v Ira Glass>Did you feel sorry for her?
<v Ira Glass>你为她感到难过吗？

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:05.710
<v Elna Baker>I felt concerned for her.
<v Elna Baker>我为她感到担心。

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:06.980
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:28:06.980 --> 00:28:19.100
<v Elna Baker>And-- and then she took out her purse. And she reached into her purse, and then she gripped onto something. And I remember thinking like, oh no, like, what-- the way-- like, if that--
<v Elna Baker>然后她拿出了她的钱包。她把手伸进钱包里，然后抓住了一个东西。我记得我当时心想，哦不，像，如果那——像——

00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:20.450
<v Ira Glass>Oh, like, it's like a gun or something.
<v Ira Glass>哦，像，它是枪什么的。

00:28:20.450 --> 00:28:31.190
<v Elna Baker>Right, gun, or a knife or something. I remember my heart just dropping. And then she pulled out a hairbrush. And then she just started brushing her hair, like, at the foot of my bed.
<v Elna Baker>对，枪，或刀什么的。我记得我的心沉了下去。然后她掏出一把梳子。然后她就开始在我的床脚梳头发。

00:28:31.190 --> 00:28:31.990
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:28:31.990 --> 00:28:38.750
<v Elna Baker>And-- and then at that point, I was like, this is really weird. Like, this is like--
<v Elna Baker>在那一刻，我就像，这真的很奇怪。像，这就像——

00:28:38.750 --> 00:28:40.310
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:28:40.310 --> 00:29:00.190
<v Elna Baker>And I don't think she's going to leave. And then she sat on the vanity in front of the mirror, at the desk. And she took her earrings off. And--
<v Elna Baker>我不认为她要离开。然后她坐在镜子前的梳妆台上，在桌子旁。她取下了耳环。而且——

00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:01.210
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wow.
<v Ira Glass>哦，哇。

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:06.800
<v Elna Baker>And she started like-- she took her shoes off. She took her coat off. She was just like--
<v Elna Baker>她开始像——她脱掉鞋子。她脱掉外套。她就像——

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:07.720
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wow.
<v Ira Glass>哦，哇。

00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:11.750
<v Elna Baker>--making herself comfortable to like, be here now.
<v Elna Baker>——让自己舒服地像，现在要待在这里。

00:29:11.750 --> 00:29:12.310
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:29:12.310 --> 00:29:39.420
<v Elna Baker>And so I let her do all that, and was sort of listening to her go on about what was wrong. And then eventually, after I felt like enough time had passed, I said, we have to wake up early to go to school tomorrow. And we're really tired, so I think we should probably go back to bed now.
<v Elna Baker>所以我让她做了所有这些，有点听着她继续说哪里出了问题。最后，在我觉得时间足够了之后，我说，明天我们要早起上学。我们真的很累，所以我想我们现在应该上床睡觉了。

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:55.550
<v Elna Baker>And she was like, I know, I know. I'm sorry, I'm sorry. I'm sorry I'm here. I'm here. I'm sorry I'm here. And I said, no, it's totally fine that you're here-- almost like, you know, thank you for being here. But do you mind leaving now?
<v Elna Baker>她说，我知道，我知道。对不起，对不起。对不起我在这里。我在这里。对不起我在这里。我说，不，你在这里完全没问题——几乎像，你知道，谢谢你在这里。但你介意现在离开吗？

00:29:55.550 --> 00:30:07.770
<v Elna Baker>And so I very slowly stood up and walked over to the door. And every moment was like making sure I was safe. And then I open the door.
<v Elna Baker>所以我非常缓慢地站起来，走到门口。每一刻都像在确保我是安全的。然后我打开门。

00:30:07.770 --> 00:30:26.410
<v Elna Baker>And she said, OK, OK. And she put her shoes on, and then she started to leave. And just as she was about to leave, I said, oh, you forgot your earrings. And then she was like-- started crying all over again, like it was this huge thing, walked over to get the earrings, and then sat back down on the bed.
<v Elna Baker>她说，好的，好的。她穿上鞋子，然后开始离开。就在她要离开的时候，我说，哦，你忘了你的耳环。然后她就像——开始又哭起来了，像这是一件大事，走过来拿耳环，然后又在床上坐了下来。

00:30:26.410 --> 00:30:49.750
<v Elna Baker>And I remember thinking like, goddamn, why did I bring up the earrings? We were so close to being safe. So then the whole thing-- basically, the whole thing started all over again, where I had to like, listen to her cry, calm her down. And then finally, I'm like, you know, we have to wake up early to go to school. Do you mind leaving?
<v Elna Baker>我记得我想像，该死，我为什么要提耳环？我们离安全那么近。所以整个事情——基本上，整个事情又重新开始了，我不得不像，听她哭，让她平静下来。最后，我说，你知道，我们明天要早起上学。你介意离开吗？

00:30:49.750 --> 00:31:08.450
<v Elna Baker>And I walked her to the door. And she walked out of the door. And then as soon as she was out of the door, I just pushed the door as hard as I could to close it. And when I pushed it, like basically slammed it shut, she screamed at the top of her lungs.
<v Elna Baker>我送她到门口。她走出门外。一出门，我就用力推门想关上它。当我推门的时候，像基本上把它重重地关上了，她放声尖叫。

00:31:08.450 --> 00:31:10.610
<v Ira Glass>And is there a bolt or something on the door?
<v Ira Glass>门上有什么插销之类的吗？

00:31:10.610 --> 00:31:12.710
<v Elna Baker>I think I did bolt it after I slammed it shut.
<v Elna Baker>我想我在重重地关上门后确实锁上了。

00:31:12.710 --> 00:31:13.220
<v Ira Glass>Uh-huh.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:21.470
<v Elna Baker>And then I heard her screaming in the hallway. And then she ran down the hallway, screaming. And then she ran back towards the door, screaming. And--
<v Elna Baker>然后我听到她在走廊里尖叫。她沿着走廊跑，尖叫着。然后她跑回门边，尖叫着。而且——

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:22.580
<v Ira Glass>That must have been terrifying.
<v Ira Glass>那一定很可怕。

00:31:22.580 --> 00:31:33.660
<v Elna Baker>It was so scary. And then-- then it got-- was quiet. And then I heard this loud-- she's like banging on the door, like as hard as she can, right?
<v Elna Baker>那真的很可怕。然后——然后就——安静了。然后我听到这个巨大的——她像在砰砰地敲门，像她能用的最大力气，对吧？

00:31:33.660 --> 00:31:42.080
<v Elna Baker>And then I hear a voice, and it's my dad. And my dad's like, open the door. Open the door. And I realize it's not her banging on the door.
<v Elna Baker>然后我听到一个声音，是我爸爸。我爸爸像，开门。开门。我意识到那不是她在敲门。

00:31:42.080 --> 00:31:54.560
<v Elna Baker>So I open the door. And my dad's standing there, and he's like, did you hear someone screaming in the hallway? So then I tell him this whole story.
<v Elna Baker>所以我开门。我爸爸站在那里，他说，你听到走廊里有人尖叫了吗？所以我告诉了他整个故事。

00:31:54.560 --> 00:32:01.280
<v Elna Baker>I'm like, there was this woman. She was in our room. I tell him all the details. And he is furious. He was so mad.
<v Elna Baker>我就像，有个女人。她在我们的房间里。我告诉了他所有细节。他非常愤怒。他太生气了。

00:32:01.280 --> 00:32:09.380
<v Elna Baker>And then he told us to stay there, shut the door, don't open the door. And then he went downstairs and talked to the hotel about it.
<v Elna Baker>然后他叫我们待在那里，关上门，不要开门。然后他下楼和酒店谈论了这件事。

00:32:09.380 --> 00:32:11.060
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, what did he learn?
<v Ira Glass>是的，他了解到了什么？

00:32:11.060 --> 00:32:20.300
<v Elna Baker>They tell him that she had been outside. She was crying. They asked if she was a guest there. She said yes, her name was Elena Baker. And so they looked up the name.
<v Elna Baker>他们告诉他她刚才在外面。她在哭。他们问她是不是那里的客人。她说是的，她的名字叫埃尔娜·贝克。所以他们查了这个名字。

00:32:20.300 --> 00:32:21.290
<v Ira Glass>Elena Baker?
<v Ira Glass>埃尔娜·贝克？

00:32:21.290 --> 00:32:22.190
<v Elna Baker>Elena Baker, yeah.
<v Elna Baker>埃尔娜·贝克，是的。

00:32:22.190 --> 00:32:23.450
<v Ira Glass>And your name is Elna Baker.
<v Ira Glass>而你的名字是埃尔娜·贝克。

00:32:23.450 --> 00:32:23.930
<v Elna Baker>Yes.
<v Elna Baker>是的。

00:32:23.930 --> 00:32:24.530
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:29.390
<v Elna Baker>But I don't believe this part of the story. I think someone at the hotel just said this to him to cover.
<v Elna Baker>但我不太相信故事的这一部分。我认为酒店里的某个人只是为了掩盖而对他说了这个。

00:32:29.390 --> 00:32:30.830
<v Ira Glass>And then they gave her a key?
<v Ira Glass>然后他们给了她一把钥匙？

00:32:30.830 --> 00:32:31.660
<v Elna Baker>And then they gave her a key.
<v Elna Baker>然后他们给了她一把钥匙。

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:32.360
<v Ira Glass>Uh-huh.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:32:32.360 --> 00:32:44.800
<v Elna Baker>Yeah. And so the hotel offered him, you know, the whole stay for free, everything for free. And he's like, I don't care about your money. I want you guys to do a formal investigation as to how this happened.
<v Elna Baker>是的。所以酒店提出给他，你知道，整个住宿免费，所有东西都免费。他像，我不关心你的钱。我想要你们对这件事进行正式调查，弄清楚这是怎么发生的。

00:32:44.800 --> 00:32:47.750
<v Ira Glass>Wow, that's such a white-man-takes-charge move.
<v Ira Glass>哇，这是一个很有“白人负责”举动的举动。

00:32:47.750 --> 00:32:49.460
<v Elna Baker>My dad's not white.
<v Elna Baker>我爸爸不是白人。

00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:53.690
<v Ira Glass>Oh, point taken. [LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>哦，重点已收到。[笑]

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:55.380
<v Elna Baker>He's a Hispanic man.
<v Elna Baker>他是一个西班牙裔男人。

00:32:55.380 --> 00:33:03.080
<v Ira Glass>I was just thinking, you just never hear like, somebody be like, I demand a formal investigation, you know what I mean? It's like he's a member of Congress or something, you know?
<v Ira Glass>我只是在想，你从来没听过像，有人会像，我要求进行正式调查，你知道我的意思吗？就像他是国会议员什么的，你懂吗？

00:33:03.080 --> 00:33:03.590
<v Elna Baker>Yeah, yeah.
<v Elna Baker>对，对。

00:33:03.590 --> 00:33:04.180
<v Ira Glass>All right, anyway--
<v Ira Glass>好的，不管怎样——

00:33:04.180 --> 00:33:07.490
<v Elna Baker>But that's what-- I mean, that's totally how my dad would-- like, that's my dad.
<v Elna Baker>但这就是——意思是，这完全是我爸爸会做的——像，那就是我爸爸。

00:33:07.490 --> 00:33:09.230
<v Ira Glass>Point taken. OK.
<v Ira Glass>重点已收到。好的。

00:33:09.230 --> 00:33:14.210
<v Elna Baker>And so they do a formal investigation, which they then send to my dad. And--
<v Elna Baker>所以他们确实进行了正式调查，后来他们发给我爸爸。而且——

00:33:14.210 --> 00:33:15.890
<v Ira Glass>Long after you've like, gone from the hotel.
<v Ira Glass>在你离开酒店很久之后。

00:33:15.890 --> 00:33:21.980
<v Elna Baker>Exactly. And in it, there's a picture of the woman. And then there's her history.
<v Elna Baker>正是如此。在里面，有一张那个女人的照片。还有她的历史。

00:33:21.980 --> 00:33:36.830
<v Elna Baker>She had been in a psychiatric hospital and escaped that night, come to the hotel. And she had a long history of breaking into people's homes. And he's not certain about this part, so I don't--
<v Elna Baker>她曾住过精神病院，那天晚上逃了出来，来到了酒店。她有很长的入室行窃历史。他不确定这一部分，所以我不——

00:33:36.830 --> 00:33:37.820
<v Ira Glass>He doesn't remember it?
<v Ira Glass>他不记得了？

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:43.850
<v Elna Baker>He doesn't remember, but he says that he remembers there being-- she had attacked people when she'd broken into their homes.
<v Elna Baker>他不记得了，但他说是他记得有——她在闯入别人家时袭击过别人。

00:33:43.850 --> 00:33:44.870
<v Ira Glass>That's what he remembers?
<v Ira Glass>那是他记得的？

00:33:44.870 --> 00:33:45.890
<v Elna Baker>That's what he remembers.
<v Elna Baker>那是他记得的。

00:33:45.890 --> 00:33:46.190
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:33:46.190 --> 00:33:53.620
<v Elna Baker>Yeah.
<v Elna Baker>是的。

00:33:53.620 --> 00:34:18.030
<v Ira Glass>That part of the story-- the investigation, who the woman was-- Elna only found that out a couple of years ago when she asked her dad about it. At the time, back when she was 14, her parents kept all that from her and her brother and sisters. They didn't want them to dwell on it or make them afraid of the world. They didn't want that evening to become a bigger deal than it already was. And Elna says today, that worked.
<v Ira Glass>故事的那部分——调查，那个女人是谁——埃尔娜也是几年前问她爸爸时才知道的。当时，在她14岁的时候，她的父母把这一切都瞒着她和她的兄弟姐妹。他们不想让他们纠结于此，也不想让他们对这个世界感到恐惧。他们不想让那个晚上变成比它已经发生的事情更严重的问题。埃尔娜今天说，这奏效了。

00:34:18.030 --> 00:34:25.940
<v Elna Baker>And it's funny, I've-- even as I've told this story over the years, people are like, oh, that's so traumatic, or that's crazy. And I don't think of it like that.
<v Elna Baker>而且很有趣，我——即使我多年来讲述这个故事，人们也像，哦，那太创伤了，或者是疯了。我不这么想。

00:34:25.940 --> 00:34:27.270
<v Ira Glass>How so?
<v Ira Glass>怎么说？

00:34:27.270 --> 00:34:44.990
<v Elna Baker>It just-- it just was sort of like a-- because nothing bad happened, it has no category for me, if that makes sense. It's been easier-- it was easier to just think like, oh, this one-- this weird thing happened one night when we were in a hotel.
<v Elna Baker>它只是——它只是像是一个——因为没有发生坏事，它对我没有分类，如果这有意义的话。这更容易——更容易只认为是像，哦，这一件——这件奇怪的事情发生在我们住酒店的一个晚上。

00:34:44.990 --> 00:34:48.740
<v Ira Glass>In retrospect, would you have preferred if they had told you back when you were 14?
<v Ira Glass>回顾过去，如果你14岁的时候他们告诉了你，你会更喜欢吗？

00:34:48.740 --> 00:34:53.610
<v Elna Baker>No, I think it's good that I got to live in a world that I think is safe.
<v Elna Baker>不，我认为好在我能生活在一个我认为是安全的世界里。

00:34:53.610 --> 00:35:20.210
<v Ira Glass>On the other hand, she says, if they had made clear how much danger she was in, the family's memory of it and her memory of it wouldn't be of this weird event with no category, this random thing that happened one night. Instead, it would fit squarely into the category called victory-- solid win for 14-year-old girl in a big, religious family, when she got that stranger out of their hotel room without anybody getting hurt. And Elna says she would have liked that win.
<v Ira Glass>另一方面，她说，如果他们清楚地表明她处于多大的危险之中，家人对它的记忆和她的记忆就不会是这个没有分类的怪事，这个一个晚上发生的随机事情。相反，它会直接落入被称为“胜利”的类别——一个巨大的、虔诚的家庭中14岁女孩的坚实胜利，当她把那个陌生人赶出他们的酒店房间而没有人受伤时。埃尔娜说她会喜欢那个胜利。

00:35:20.210 --> 00:35:41.890
<v Ira Glass>When you're trapped inside a room with an unpredictable person, really, all you've got is your wits, and your ability to read the situation, and your judgment at guessing what will work. Nice to show everybody that even a kid can make the right call sometimes, if they pay attention closely enough.
<v Ira Glass>当你被困在一个不可预测的人的房间里时，实际上，你所拥有的只有你的智慧，你阅读情况的能力，以及你猜测什么会起作用的判断力。很高兴向大家展示，即使是一个孩子，如果他们足够仔细地观察，有时也能做出正确的决定。

00:35:41.890 --> 00:35:51.120
<v David Kestenbaum>Elna Baker, one of the producers of our show, talking to Ira Glass.
<v David Kestenbaum>埃尔娜·贝克，我们节目的制作人之一，正在和艾拉·格拉斯交谈。

00:35:51.120 --> 00:36:11.710
<v David Kestenbaum>Act three-- You Can't Go Your Own Way. In her memoir, In the Dream House, Carmen Maria Machado wrote this chapter that's set up like a choose your own adventure story, which I just thought was so smart when I read it. In it, she imagines different choices she could have made with a partner that she's in a bad relationship with-- an abusive relationship.
<v David Kestenbaum>第三幕——“你不能走自己的路”。在她的回忆录《梦想之屋》（In the Dream House）中，卡门·玛丽亚·马查多写了这一章，其设置就像一个“选择你自己的冒险”故事，我在阅读时觉得这太聪明了。在书中，她想象了如果她和一段糟糕的关系中的伴侣——一段虐待关系——在一起时，她本可以做出的不同选择。

00:36:11.710 --> 00:36:28.550
<v David Kestenbaum>And one of the things it captures is just the mental energy that somebody in that situation can end up putting into trying to figure out what they should do or not do to avoid setting off their partner. A heads up that this excerpt includes a scene with sexual content. It's read for us by actor Zoe Winters.
<v David Kestenbaum>它捕捉到的其中一点，是一个处于那种境地的人最终可能投入的心理能量，去试图弄清楚他们应该做什么或不应该做什么，以避免触怒他们的伴侣。提醒一下，这段节选包含性内容。由演员佐伊·温特斯为我们朗读。

