<v Zoe Winters>第七页——早餐。你炒了一些鸡蛋，做了些烤面包。她机械地吃着，把盘子留在桌上。清理那个，她说，然后去卧室穿衣服。

00:41:08.570 --> 00:41:48.840
<v Zoe Winters>If you do as you are told, go to page eight. If you tell her to do it herself, go to page five. If you stare mutely at the dirty plate, and all you can think about is Clara Barton, the feminist icon of your youth, who had to teach herself how to be a nurse, and endured abuse from men telling her what to do at every turn, and you remember being so angry, and running to your parents and asking them if women still got told what was right or proper, and your mom said yes, and your dad said no, and you, for the first time, had an inkling of how complicated and terrible the world was, go to page 10.
<v Zoe Winters>如果你照做，转到第八页。如果你告诉她自己做，转到第五页。如果你呆滞地盯着脏盘子，你所能想到的只有克拉拉·巴顿，你年轻时的女权主义偶像，她必须自学如何成为护士，并忍受男人的虐待，在每一个转弯处告诉她做什么，你记得当时非常生气，跑去问你父母女性是否仍然被告知什么是正确或适当的，你妈妈说是的，你爸爸说不，而你，第一次隐约感觉到世界是多么复杂和可怕，转到第十页。

00:41:48.840 --> 00:42:17.730
<v Zoe Winters>Page 10-- that night, she [MUTED] you as you lie there mutely, praying for it to be over, praying she won't notice you're gone. You have voided your body so many times by now that it is force of habit, reflexive as a sigh. It reminds you of your first boyfriend, who [MUTED] you while watching porn, how he rutted and rutted, and then every so often, lifted the remote to rewind something you couldn't see.
<v Zoe Winters>第十页——那天晚上，她[消音]你，当你呆滞地躺在那里，祈祷它早点结束，祈祷她不会注意到你离开了。你已经多次清空了你的身体，以至于这成了习惯，像叹息一样条件反射。它让我想起了我的第一个男朋友，他一边看色情片一边[消音]我，他怎么不断地摩擦，然后每隔一段时间，举起遥控器倒回一些你看不到的东西。

00:42:17.730 --> 00:42:51.270
<v Zoe Winters>Once, you turned your head over the lip of the bed and saw a tangle of upside-down limbs, and your brain couldn't make sense of them. You never looked again. You would just lie there silently, watching his face move over you. It was like being unfolded beneath the yawn of the planetarium as a kid-- the sped-up rotation of the Earth, the movement of the stars, the constellations melting into and out of being.
<v Zoe Winters>有一次，你把头伸过床沿，看到了倒置的四肢纠缠在一起，你的大脑无法理解它们。你再也没有看过。你只会默默地躺在那里，看着他的脸在你身上移动。就像小时候被展开在天文馆的巨大穹顶下一样——地球旋转的加速，星星的运动，星座融化成存在和不存在。

00:42:51.270 --> 00:43:08.940
<v Zoe Winters>You shudder and moan with precision. She turns off the lights. You watch the darkness until the darkness leaves you, or you leave it.
<v Zoe Winters>你颤抖并精确地呻吟。她关掉灯。你看着黑暗，直到黑暗离开你，或者你离开它。

00:43:08.940 --> 00:43:19.920
<v Zoe Winters>To sleep, go to page 14. To dream about the present, go to page 13. To dream about the future, go to page 12.
<v Zoe Winters>要睡觉，转到第十四页。要梦到过去的你，转到第十一页。要梦到现在的你，转到第十三页。

00:43:19.920 --> 00:43:28.690
<v Zoe Winters>To dream about the past, go to page 11. I want to dream about the past. I choose page 11.
<v Zoe Winters>要梦到过去的你，转到第十一页。我想梦到过去。我选择第十一页。

00:43:28.690 --> 00:44:02.560
<v Zoe Winters>Page 11-- the first time it happened. The first time she yelled at you so much you were crying within 30 seconds from waking-- a record. She said, the first 10 minutes of the day, I'm not responsible for anything I say. This struck you as poetic. You even wrote it down, sure you would find a place for it in a book, maybe. Go to page 14.
<v Zoe Winters>第十一页——第一次发生这件事。第一次她对你大喊大叫，以至于你从醒来开始就在30秒内哭了——一个记录。她说，一天的前10分钟，我对我说的一切都不负责。这让你觉得很有诗意。你甚至把它写了下来，确信你会为它找到一个位置，也许在一本书里。转到第十四页。

00:44:02.560 --> 00:44:24.780
<v Zoe Winters>Page 9-- you shouldn't be on this page. There's no way to get here from the choices given to you. Do you think that, by flipping through this chapter linearly, you'd find some kind of relief? Don't you get it? All of this shit already happened. And you can't make it not happen, no matter what you do.
<v Zoe Winters>第九页——你不应该在这页。从给你提供的选择中，没有办法来到这里。你认为通过线性翻阅这一章，你会找到某种解脱吗？你还不明白吗？所有这些狗屎都已经发生了。无论你做什么，你都无法让它不发生。

00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:36.320
<v Zoe Winters>Do you want a picture of a fawn? Will that help? OK, here's a fawn. She is small, and dappled, and loose-legged.
<v Zoe Winters>你想要一张小鹿的照片吗？那会有帮助吗？好的，这是一只小鹿。她是小的，斑点的，腿很松。

00:44:36.320 --> 00:44:48.330
<v Zoe Winters>She hears a sound, freezes, and then bolts. She knows what to do. She knows there's somewhere safer she can be. Go to page 10.
<v Zoe Winters>她听到一个声音，冻住了，然后跑了。她知道该怎么做。她知道有一个更安全的地方她可以待着。转到第十页。

00:44:48.330 --> 00:45:06.550
<v Zoe Winters>I don't want to go to page 10. Page 6-- you shouldn't be on this page. There's no way to get here from the choices given to you. You flipped here because you got sick of the cycle.
<v Zoe Winters>我不想去第十页。第六页——你不应该在这页。从给你提供的选择中，没有办法来到这里。你翻到这里是因为你厌倦了这个循环。

00:45:06.550 --> 00:45:13.240
<v Zoe Winters>You wanted to get out. You're smarter than me. Go to page 10.
<v Zoe Winters>你想脱身。你比我聪明。转到第十页。

00:45:13.240 --> 00:45:31.910
<v Zoe Winters>Fine. I go to page 10. Page 10-- that night, she fucks you as you lie there mutely, praying for it to be over, praying she won't notice you're gone. You have voided your body so many times by now that it is force of habit, reflexive as a sigh.
<v Zoe Winters>好的。我转到第十页。第十页——那天晚上，她操你，当你呆滞地躺在那里，祈祷它早点结束，祈祷她不会注意到你离开了。你已经多次清空了你的身体，以至于这成了习惯，像叹息一样条件反射。

00:45:31.910 --> 00:45:49.700
<v Zoe Winters>It reminds you of your first-- the sped-up rotation of the Earth, the movement of the stars, the constellations melting into and out of being. You shudder and moan with precision. She turns off the lights. You watch the darkness until the darkness leaves you, or you leave it.
<v Zoe Winters>它让我想起了我的第一个——地球旋转的加速，星星的运动，星座融化成存在和不存在。你颤抖并精确地呻吟。她关掉灯。你看着黑暗，直到黑暗离开你，或者你离开它。

00:45:49.700 --> 00:45:59.400
<v Zoe Winters>To sleep, go to page 14. To dream about the past, go to page 11. To dream about the present, go to page 13.
<v Zoe Winters>若要睡觉，请翻到第14页。若要梦见过去，请翻到第11页。若要梦见现在，请翻到第13页。

00:45:59.400 --> 00:46:14.150
<v Zoe Winters>Page 13-- you shouldn't be here, but it's OK. It's a dream. She can't find you here. In a minute, you're going to wake up, and everything is going to seem like it's the same, but it's not.
<v Zoe Winters>第十三页——你不应该在这里，但没关系。这是一个梦。她在这里找不到你。一分钟后，你会醒来，一切看起来都一样，但不是这样。

00:46:14.150 --> 00:46:23.390
<v Zoe Winters>There's a way out. Are you listening to me? You can't forget when you wake up. You can't go to page 14.
<v Zoe Winters>有一条出路。你在听我说话吗？醒来时你不能忘记。你不能转到第十四页。

00:46:23.390 --> 00:46:32.060
<v Zoe Winters>I want to dream about the future. I go to page 12. Page 12-- it's going to be all right.
<v Zoe Winters>我想梦到未来。我转到第十二页。第十二页——一切都会好的。

00:46:32.060 --> 00:46:51.770
<v Zoe Winters>One day, your wife will gently adjust your arm if it touches her face at night, soothingly straightening it while kissing you. Sometimes, you will wake up just enough to notice. Other times, she'll only tell you in the morning. It's the kind of morning you could get used to. Go to page 14.
<v Zoe Winters>有一天，你的妻子会温柔地调整你的手臂，如果它在晚上碰到她的脸，舒缓地拉直它，同时吻你。有时，你会醒来刚好注意到。其他时候，她只会在早晨告诉你。这是你可以习惯的那种早晨。转到第十四页。

00:46:51.770 --> 00:47:04.400
<v Zoe Winters>Page 14-- you wake up, and the air is milky and bright. The room glows with a kind of effervescent contentment, despite the boxes, and clothes, and dishes. You think to yourself, this is the kind of morning you could get used to.
<v Zoe Winters>第十四页——你醒了，空气奶白色明亮。房间里洋溢着一种冒泡的满足感，尽管有纸箱、衣服和盘子。你心想，这是你可以习惯的那种早晨。

00:47:04.400 --> 00:47:11.480
<v Zoe Winters>When you turn over, she is staring at you. The luminous innocence of the light curdles in your stomach. You don't remember ever going from awake to afraid so quickly.
<v Zoe Winters>当你翻身时，她盯着你看。光线的明亮纯真在你的胃里凝固。你不记得曾经如此迅速地从清醒变得恐惧。

00:47:11.480 --> 00:47:16.820
<v Zoe Winters>You were moving all night, she says. Your arms and elbows touched me. You kept me awake.
<v Zoe Winters>你整晚都在动，她说。你的手臂和肘部碰到了我。你让我无法入睡。

00:47:16.820 --> 00:47:37.160
<v Zoe Winters>If you apologize profusely, go to page two. If you tell her to wake you up next time your elbows touch her in your sleep, go to page three. If you toss back the blankets from your body and hit the floor with both feet, and tear through the house like it's Pamplona, and when you get to the driveway, your car keys are already in your hand, and you drive away with a theatrical squeal of the tires, never to return again, go to page 15.
<v Zoe Winters>如果你深表歉意，转到第二页。如果你告诉她下次你的肘部在睡梦中碰到她时叫醒你，转到第三页。如果你从身上踢开毯子，两只脚着地，像在潘普洛纳一样在房子里横冲直撞，当你到达车道时，车钥匙已经在你手里，你带着剧烈的轮胎摩擦声开车离开，再也没有回来，转到第十五页。

00:47:37.160 --> 00:47:53.290
<v Zoe Winters>Page 15-- that's not how it happened, but OK, we can pretend. I'll give it to you, just this once. Turn to page 16.
<v Zoe Winters>第十五页——事情不是那样发生的，但好吧，我们可以假装。我给你这个机会，就这一次。翻到第十六页。

00:47:53.290 --> 00:48:32.300
<v Zoe Winters>Page 16-- an end. In the pit of it, you fantasize about dying-- tripping on a sidewalk, and stumbling into the path of an oncoming car, a gas leak silently offing you in your sleep, a machete-wielding madman on public transit, falling down the stairs, but drunk, so you flop, limb over limb, like a marionette, and feel no pain-- anything to make it stop. You have forgotten that leaving is an option.
<v Zoe Winters>第十六页——一个结局。在深渊中，你幻想死亡——在人行道上绊倒，跌入迎面而来的车道，睡觉时漏出的煤气静静地杀死你，公共交通工具上的持刀疯子，从楼梯上跌倒，但喝醉了，所以你像木偶一样肢体瘫软，感觉不到痛苦——任何事情只要能让它停止。你已经忘记离开是一个选择。

00:48:32.300 --> 00:48:48.270
<v David Kestenbaum>Zoe Winters reading a chapter from Carmen Maria Machado's memoir, In the Dream House. Carmen has a comic series out now called The Low, Low Woods, from DC Comics. You can see Zoe Winters in season three of HBO'S Succession, which is out in October.
<v David Kestenbaum>佐伊·温特斯朗读卡门·玛丽亚·马查多回忆录《梦想之屋》中的一章。卡门现在有一部名为《低，低林》（The Low, Low Woods）的漫画系列，由DC漫画出版。你可以在HBO的《继承之战》第三季中看到佐伊·温特斯，该剧于10月播出。

00:48:48.270 --> 00:49:07.180
<v David Kestenbaum>Act four-- Blue Kid on the Block. This last story is about what happens after you get out-- in this particular case, when you get out of Rochester, New York. The kid in this story, who we're going to call Leo-- it's not his real name-- his family had moved to an entirely different state. And he was not happy about it.
<v David Kestenbaum>第四幕——“街区的蓝色小孩”。最后一个故事是关于你脱身之后发生的事情——在这个特殊的例子中，当你离开纽约州罗切斯特的时候。在这个故事里，我们称之为“利奥”（Leo）的孩子——这不是他的真名——他的家人搬到了一个完全不同的州。而他对此并不高兴。

00:49:07.180 --> 00:49:26.650
<v David Kestenbaum>At the time, Leo was just starting seventh grade. He was having a harder time finding his place in middle school than other middle school kids, because, you know, he was new to town, but also because Leo still loved Rochester. He loved his school there. He loved his friends. Suddenly, everything about his life was different, and according to Leo, much worse.
<v David Kestenbaum>当时，利奥刚开始读七年级。他比其他初中生更难在学校找到自己的位置，因为，你知道，他是镇上的新人，但也因为利奥仍然热爱罗切斯特。他热爱他在那里的学校。他热爱他的朋友。突然之间，他生活的一切都不同了，而且根据利奥的说法，更糟了。

00:49:26.650 --> 00:49:38.690
<v David Kestenbaum>The signature on his Gmail account now read, Rochester is much better, in big, red letters. Sarah Koenig knows Leo and his parents. She visited them at home to see how Leo is doing.
<v David Kestenbaum>他Gmail账户上的签名现在写着，罗切斯特好得多，用的是大大的红字。莎拉·柯尼希认识利奥和他的父母。她到他们家拜访，看看利奥过得怎么样。

00:49:38.690 --> 00:49:55.820
<v Sarah Koenig>This is how much Leo does not want to be here-- when I got to his house, he was on the sofa with a laptop, investigating Greyhound bus schedules. His mother was going to drive him and his sister back to Rochester for the Columbus Day weekend, but not until Saturday morning. And Leo wanted to get there Friday for more time with his friends.
<v Sarah Koenig>这就是利奥有多不想待在这里的程度——当我到达他家时，他正坐在沙发上用笔记本电脑，调查灰狗巴士的时间表。他妈妈要在哥伦布日周末带他和妹妹回罗切斯特，但要到周六早上才走。而利奥想在周五到达那里，好和朋友多待一会儿。

00:49:55.820 --> 00:50:00.600
<v Sarah Koenig>A multi-stage negotiation followed with his dad. For one thing, a bus ticket costs money.
<v Sarah Koenig>随后他和他爸爸进行了多阶段的谈判。首先，一张巴士票要钱。

00:50:00.600 --> 00:50:02.300
<v Leo>That's not the-- that's not a problem. [? I have that money-- ?]
<v Leo>那不是——那不是问题。[？我有那笔钱——？]

00:50:02.300 --> 00:50:04.010
<v Leo's Dad>Not for you. No, that wasn't a problem for you.
<v Leo's Dad>不是你。不，那对你来说不是问题。

00:50:04.010 --> 00:50:05.360
<v Leo>No, but--
<v Leo>不，但是——

00:50:05.360 --> 00:50:06.120
<v Leo's Dad>There isn't one, right?
<v Leo's Dad>没有，对吧？

00:50:06.120 --> 00:50:10.580
<v Leo>There isn't one-- not at 6:15 in the morning. And you won't let me miss school on Friday.
<v Leo>没有——早上6:15没有。而且你不会让我周五错过上学。

00:50:10.580 --> 00:50:16.160
<v Leo's Dad>The bus takes eight hours. Even if you left at noon, you'd be at-- are gonna do the math? That's excellent.
<v Leo's Dad>巴士要走八个小时。即使你中午走，你也会在——是要做数学计算吗？那很好。

00:50:16.160 --> 00:50:35.700
<v Sarah Koenig>Never mind that the bus takes twice as long as driving, and never mind that Leo would be alone, and have to change buses, and that his parents had no intention of letting him go through with this plan. But it's the kind of negotiation you indulge when your kid is miserable. And Leo is miserable. He told me right away he was, and that he had been from the moment he got here in August.
<v Sarah Koenig>别管巴士需要的时间是开车两倍，别管利奥会一个人，必须换乘巴士，也别管他父母根本没打算让他实行这个计划。但这是当你孩子痛苦时你纵容的那种谈判。而且利奥非常痛苦。他立刻告诉了我，他从八月份来到这里的那一刻起就是如此。

00:50:35.700 --> 00:50:42.990
<v Sarah Koenig>Is this the first time you've-- in your life where you felt like you've been sad about something for this long?
<v Sarah Koenig>这是你生活中第一次感到因为某事难过这么久吗？

00:50:42.990 --> 00:50:48.490
<v Leo>I think so, except for maybe when my other cat died-- my old cat.
<v Leo>我想是吧，除了可能是我另一只猫死的时候——我以前的那只猫。

00:50:48.490 --> 00:50:51.050
<v Sarah Koenig>And does this feel worse than that?
<v Sarah Koenig>这感觉比那次更糟吗？

00:50:51.050 --> 00:50:52.060
<v Leo>Yes.
<v Leo>是的。

00:50:52.060 --> 00:50:52.940
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh, really?
<v Sarah Koenig>哦，真的吗？

00:50:52.940 --> 00:51:13.190
<v Leo>Mhm. I've never had long periods of sad until now. I don't know. I don't know anyone here, really. And I think it's just everything in general that's overwhelming.
<v Leo>嗯哼。我直到现在才有过长时间的难过。我不知道。我真的不认识这里的人。我认为这只是普遍的一切都让人不知所措。

00:51:13.190 --> 00:51:23.900
<v Sarah Koenig>Back in Rochester, Leo had known all his best friends since kindergarten or before. They played together at school, after school, on weekends. Everybody knew everybody. Everything was comfortable.
<v Sarah Koenig>回到罗切斯特，利奥从幼儿园或更早的时候就认识了他所有的好朋友。他们在学校、放学后、周末一起玩。每个人都认识每个人。一切都很舒服。

00:51:23.900 --> 00:51:41.150
<v Sarah Koenig>And as a sixth grader in his old school, he and his friends were at the top of the heap. They wore green sashes in the morning, and got to be door monitors for the younger kids coming into the building. So imagine now, Leo takes a school bus for the first time to his new school, a sprawling one-story building full of sixth, seventh, and eighth graders.
<v Sarah Koenig>而且作为他老学校的六年级学生，他和他的朋友们处于顶端。他们早上戴着绿色腰带，成为低年级学生进入大楼的门卫。所以现在想象一下，利奥第一次坐校车去他的新学校，一栋庞大的单层建筑，满是六年级、七年级和八年级学生。

00:51:41.150 --> 00:51:48.320
<v Sarah Koenig>Leo's small for his age, only a hair taller than his sister, who's only nine years old. On day one, he knows exactly no one.
<v Sarah Koenig>利奥年纪小，只比他九岁的妹妹高一点点。第一天，他谁也不认识。

00:51:48.320 --> 00:52:06.580
<v Leo>It was much louder than my old school, much louder. People talking, people closing and opening lockers. People walking-- it was just noise. Just older kids, more kids, because my old school was tiny.
<v Leo>它比我老学校吵多了，吵多了。人们说话，人们关开储物柜。人们走路——全是噪音。只是大孩子，更多的孩子，因为我老学校太小了。

00:52:06.580 --> 00:52:28.610
<v Sarah Koenig>Leo is between. He's old enough to decipher Greyhound bus schedules, but not old enough to actually travel on one by himself, and old enough to know that, if he's going to survive in middle school, he has to make friends, but that making those friends stands to be significantly more complicated than it was back in Rochester. Leo's sister, Auden, is in fourth grade. He says she's not having as hard a time of it.
<v Sarah Koenig>利奥处于中间。他足够大到能破解灰狗巴士的时间表，但又不够大到能自己一个人乘坐，而且足够大到知道如果他要在初中生存，他就必须交朋友，但交朋友看起来要比在罗切斯特复杂得多。利奥的妹妹奥登（Auden）读四年级。他说她没那么难过。

00:52:28.610 --> 00:52:39.670
<v Leo>She hasn't been alive as long. She hasn't made as deep connections. And she's already met some-- a friend in her school. I think it's easier to make friends in elementary school than in middle school.
<v Leo>她活的时间还没那么长。她还没建立那么深的联系。而且她已经遇到了一些——在学校的一个朋友。我认为小学比初中更容易交朋友。

00:52:39.670 --> 00:52:41.020
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh, really? Why?
<v Sarah Koenig>哦，真的吗？为什么？

00:52:41.020 --> 00:52:44.620
<v Leo>Because people care less about who you are.
<v Leo>因为人们不太在乎你是谁。

00:52:44.620 --> 00:52:46.150
<v Sarah Koenig>What do you mean by that?
<v Sarah Koenig>你那是什么意思？

00:52:46.150 --> 00:53:10.560
<v Leo>The older you get, the more you judge people on their looks, their background, their-- how they act, like, what cool is for kids. Because in kindergarten, you could just walk up to someone and say, do you want to be my friend? And they would-- then, that would be it. But it's harder.
<v Leo>你年纪越大，你就越根据外表、背景、他们——他们如何行事来评价人，像，什么对孩子来说是酷的。因为在幼儿园，你只要走到某人面前说，你想成为我的朋友吗？他们就会——那么，这就行了。但很难。

00:53:10.560 --> 00:53:24.680
<v Leo>I just think people are more wary before they open up. They like-- I don't know, in first grade, if I met someone, I wouldn't really care who they were. I would just care if they were nice or not.
<v Leo>我只是认为人们在敞开心扉之前更谨慎。他们喜欢——我不知道，在一年级，如果我见到某人，我真的不在乎他们是谁。我只关心他们是否善良。

00:53:24.680 --> 00:53:41.840
<v Sarah Koenig>The day I interviewed him, Leo had had a couple of breakthroughs. He emailed his parents during the school day to tell them the first piece of good news they'd had from him. Until that day, the emails had mostly been three desperate words-- I feel awful. Without even a period at the end to make the feeling finite, it was an endless awful.
<v Sarah Koenig>我采访他的那天，利奥取得了一些突破。他在上学期间给父母发电子邮件，告诉他们他从他那里听到的第一个好消息。在那天之前，邮件大多是三个绝望的词——我感觉很糟糕。甚至连结尾的句号都没有，让那种感觉显得没完没了，真是无尽的糟糕。

00:53:41.840 --> 00:54:00.560
<v Sarah Koenig>But on this Thursday, he wrote to tell them the mashed potatoes they serve in the cafeteria were great, followed by four exclamation points. Second-- and this was the big news-- he asked another kid if he wanted to come over. Leo told me he thought about it first for a few days, then finally emailed the boy, whose name is Devin, another seventh grader.
<v Sarah Koenig>但在这周四，他写信告诉他们自助餐厅供应的土豆泥很棒，后面跟着四个感叹号。第二——这是大新闻——他问另一个孩子是否想过来。利奥告诉我想了好几天，最后才发邮件给那个男孩，他的名字叫德文（Devin），也是七年级学生。

00:54:00.560 --> 00:54:09.320
<v Sarah Koenig>But now, Leo didn't want to call Devin's house to finalize a plan. He dreaded the awkwardness of the phone. So his dad called Devin's parents, introduced himself as Leo's father.
<v Sarah Koenig>但现在，利奥不想打电话给德文家来确定计划。他害怕电话的尴尬。所以他爸爸给德文的父母打了电话，介绍了自己是利奥的父亲。

00:54:09.320 --> 00:54:23.640
<v Leo's Dad>--dad. He goes to school with your son, Evan. And we were-- Devin-- I'm so sorry, Devin. I'm getting the evil look from my son. I apologize. But we're hoping Devin can come over on Sunday. Thanks. I panicked.
<v Leo's Dad>——爸爸。他和你的儿子埃文（Evan）一起上学。我们当时——德文——我真的很抱歉，德文。我从我儿子那里得到了邪恶的目光。我道歉。但我们希望德文周日能过来。谢谢。我慌了。

00:54:23.640 --> 00:54:26.960
<v Leo>Dad, you fool! [LAUGHS]
<v Leo>爸爸，你这个傻瓜！[笑]

00:54:26.960 --> 00:54:28.040
<v Sarah Koenig>How'd that go?
<v Sarah Koenig>进行得怎么样？

00:54:28.040 --> 00:54:28.790
<v Leo>Not well.
<v Leo>不太好。

00:54:28.790 --> 00:54:32.420
<v Leo>[LAUGHTER]
<v Leo>[笑声]

00:54:32.420 --> 00:54:33.600
<v Leo's Dad>This is my son, Freddy.
<v Leo's Dad>这是我的儿子，弗雷迪（Freddy）。

00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:35.060
<v Leo's Dad>[LAUGHTER]
<v Leo's Dad>[笑声]

00:54:35.060 --> 00:54:44.210
<v Sarah Koenig>I thought Leo might be upset about it, maybe get sulky. But here he was, cracking up, joking with his dad. It was such a relief to hear him laugh after he'd been so solemn in our interview.
<v Sarah Koenig>我以为利奥可能会为此感到不安，可能会变得闷闷不乐。但他就在这里，开怀大笑，和他爸爸开玩笑。在他采访中表现得那么严肃之后听到他的笑声真是一种解脱。

00:54:44.210 --> 00:54:53.810
<v Sarah Koenig>And I thought, this is all going to be OK. He's going to snap out of it. He's almost there. An afternoon with Devin is going to do the trick. Then, Sunday came.
<v Sarah Koenig>我心想，这一切都会好的。他会走出来的。他快到了。一个和德文待在一起的下午会奏效的。然后，周日到了。

00:54:53.810 --> 00:54:56.510
<v Devin>OK, now I have three health again.
<v Devin>好的，现在我又有三点健康了。

00:54:56.510 --> 00:54:58.850
<v Leo>I can tell your-- you could tell your wolf to attack.
<v Leo>我可以告诉你的——你可以告诉你的狼进攻。

00:54:58.850 --> 00:55:01.340
<v Devin>OK, I'm going to tell my wolf to attack, then.
<v Devin>好的，我要告诉我的狼进攻，然后。

00:55:01.340 --> 00:55:03.770
<v Sarah Koenig>Leo taught Devin how to play Dungeons and Dragons.
<v Sarah Koenig>利奥教德文怎么玩《龙与地下城》。

00:55:03.770 --> 00:55:06.440
<v Leo>And the wolf lands on its feet.
<v Leo>狼着地了。

00:55:06.440 --> 00:55:09.020
<v Devin>Yay! Go, wolfie! Go, wolfie!
<v Devin>耶！去吧，小狼！去吧，小狼！

00:55:09.020 --> 00:55:09.320
<v Leo>Mm-hmm.
<v Leo>嗯哼。

00:55:09.320 --> 00:55:13.190
<v Devin>Go, wolfie! Oh, I am so weird.
<v Devin>去吧，小狼！哦，我真是太奇怪了。

00:55:13.190 --> 00:55:20.810
<v Sarah Koenig>On the drive home, Leo and Devin talk nonstop-- a gentle, rat-a-tat one-upmanship emanating from deep inside a computer game.
<v Sarah Koenig>在回家的路上，利奥和德文不停地说话——一种温和的、急促的竞争，从电脑游戏深处散发出来。

00:55:20.810 --> 00:55:24.140
<v Leo>I am a-- I am what else, warlock?
<v Leo>我是个——我还是什么，术士？

00:55:24.140 --> 00:55:28.490
<v Devin>Warlocks are fun. Can you summon your imp yet?
<v Devin>术士很有趣。你能召唤你的小鬼了吗？

00:55:28.490 --> 00:55:29.630
<v Leo>Yeah, you start with imp.
<v Leo>是的，你从召唤小鬼开始。

00:55:29.630 --> 00:55:34.480
<v Devin>Oh, right. I like-- I had a goblin shaman.
<v Devin>哦，对。我喜欢——我以前有个地精萨满。

00:55:34.480 --> 00:55:39.670
<v Leo>I have a night elf druid. And I can turn into a cat at level eight, which is nice.
<v Leo>我有一个暗夜精灵德鲁伊。而且我8级时可以变成一只猫，这很不错。

00:55:39.670 --> 00:55:41.420
<v Devin>And at level 12, you get a bear.
<v Devin>而且在12级时，你会得到一头熊。

00:55:41.420 --> 00:55:41.670
<v Leo>Yeah.
<v Leo>是的。

00:55:41.670 --> 00:55:42.460
<v Devin>And then at level--
<v Devin>而且然后在——级时——

00:55:42.460 --> 00:55:44.530
<v Sarah Koenig>By the time they drop Devin off, they were giggling.
<v Sarah Koenig>当他们放下德文时，他们正在咯咯笑。

00:55:44.530 --> 00:55:45.040
<v Leo>--yeah, you start with--
<v Leo>——是的，你从——开始。

00:55:45.040 --> 00:55:45.400
<v Devin>That's funny.
<v Devin>那很有趣。

00:55:45.400 --> 00:55:50.220
<v Leo>Our cat responds to whatever you call him. [? And he ?] [? said, ?] his name is Pippen, but we can call him Football, and he'll respond.
<v Leo>我们的猫对你叫他什么都有反应。[？而且他说，？]他的名字叫皮蓬（Pippen），但我们可以叫他足球（Football），他会有反应。

00:55:50.220 --> 00:55:50.940
<v Leo>[LAUGHTER]
<v Leo>[笑声]

00:55:50.940 --> 00:55:51.520
<v Devin>You call him Football?
<v Devin>你叫他足球？

00:55:51.520 --> 00:55:55.160
<v Leo>Here, Football! Here, Football!
<v Leo>来，足球！来，足球！

00:55:55.160 --> 00:55:56.400
<v Leo's Dad>Thanks for coming over.
<v Leo's Dad>谢谢你过来。

00:55:56.400 --> 00:55:56.670
<v Leo>Thanks.
<v Leo>谢谢。

00:55:56.670 --> 00:55:58.020
<v Devin>Thank you for inviting me.
<v Devin>谢谢你邀请我。

00:55:58.020 --> 00:55:58.880
<v Leo>See you tomorrow.
<v Leo>明天见。

00:55:58.880 --> 00:56:01.370
<v Devin>See you.
<v Devin>再见。

00:56:01.370 --> 00:56:07.060
<v Sarah Koenig>So a perfect day, right? Leo's parents were relieved, hopeful. But no.
<v Sarah Koenig>所以是完美的一天，对吧？利奥的父母松了一口气，充满希望。但没有。

00:56:07.060 --> 00:56:11.510
<v Sarah Koenig>It's Wednesday. You had your friend over on Sunday. How'd it go?
<v Sarah Koenig>今天是周三。你周日有朋友过来。进行得怎么样？

00:56:11.510 --> 00:56:16.060
<v Leo>It went OK. Yeah, it went OK.
<v Leo>还行。是的，还可以。

00:56:16.060 --> 00:56:20.850
<v Sarah Koenig>Has it changed anything about being here?
<v Sarah Koenig>这改变了待在这里的任何事情吗？

00:56:20.850 --> 00:56:25.710
<v Leo>A little bit. I don't think very much. But a little bit, yeah.
<v Leo>一点点。我觉得不多。但一点点，是的。

00:56:25.710 --> 00:56:28.140
<v Sarah Koenig>What's changed? What's the little bit?
<v Sarah Koenig>什么改变了？那一点点是什么？

00:56:28.140 --> 00:56:42.980
<v Leo>That I know someone at school. It helps. Not all that much, but sort of, yeah.
<v Leo>我在学校认识某人了。这有帮助。没有那么多，但有点，是的。

00:56:42.980 --> 00:56:53.670
<v Sarah Koenig>And this is when I realized I'd underestimated the depth of Leo's gloom-- that he greets every morning of every school day with dread, and not because he's being bullied, or anyone's being mean to him.
<v Sarah Koenig>就是这时我意识到我低估了利奥悲伤的深度——他迎接每一个上学日的早晨都充满恐惧，并不是因为他被欺负，或者有人对他刻薄。

00:56:53.670 --> 00:57:06.740
<v Leo>I feel sick, because I know that I have the whole day ahead of me, and then I have the next day, and the next day, and the next day, and the next day, and the next day ahead of me.
<v Leo>我感到恶心，因为我知道我整天都在面前，然后我还有明天，明天，明天，明天，明天，面前的明天。

00:57:06.740 --> 00:57:09.440
<v Sarah Koenig>Does it pass once you get to school?
<v Sarah Koenig>当你去学校时会过去吗？

00:57:09.440 --> 00:57:23.780
<v Leo>No, not really. It usually increases to a climax around lunchtime. And then I-- actually, I've been throwing up recently. And then it just stays at that level of sickness until I get home.
<v Leo>不，并不是真的。通常在午餐时间增加到高潮。然后我——事实上，我最近一直在呕吐。然后那种恶心的程度就一直保持那个水平直到我回家。

00:57:23.780 --> 00:57:26.040
<v Sarah Koenig>You're kidding me. You're throwing up at school?
<v Sarah Koenig>你在开玩笑吧。你在学校呕吐？

00:57:26.040 --> 00:57:26.950
<v Leo>Yeah.
<v Leo>是的。

00:57:26.950 --> 00:57:30.530
<v Sarah Koenig>Aw, that's awful. In the bathroom, or where?
<v Sarah Koenig>啊，这太糟糕了。在洗手间，或者哪里？

00:57:30.530 --> 00:57:36.240
<v Leo>Yeah, in the bathroom. Usually at lunch, I feel really bad. And then I go to the bathroom, and I throw up.
<v Leo>是的，在洗手间。通常在午餐时，我感觉非常糟糕。然后我去洗手间，我呕吐。

00:57:36.240 --> 00:57:38.000
<v Sarah Koenig>Has that happened this week?
<v Sarah Koenig>这周发生过吗？

00:57:38.000 --> 00:57:39.950
<v Leo>Today, it did.
<v Leo>今天发生了。

00:57:39.950 --> 00:57:46.100
<v Sarah Koenig>Do you tell your parents like, I don't want to go, I don't want to go? Or do you just-- you know you have to, so you don't say anything?
<v Sarah Koenig>你告诉父母像，我不想去，我不想去吗？还是你只是——你知道你必须去，所以你什么都不说？

00:57:46.100 --> 00:57:48.440
<v Leo>No, I throw a screaming fit.
<v Leo>不，我发了歇斯底里的疯。

00:57:48.440 --> 00:57:49.830
<v Sarah Koenig>Every morning?
<v Sarah Koenig>每天早上？

00:57:49.830 --> 00:57:52.730
<v Leo>Yeah, pretty much. I didn't today.
<v Leo>是的，差不多。今天没有。

00:57:52.730 --> 00:57:55.250
<v Sarah Koenig>What was different about this morning? How come you didn't today?
<v Sarah Koenig>今天早上有什么不同？你今天为什么没发疯？

00:57:55.250 --> 00:58:06.380
<v Leo>I felt resigned. I mean, I knew that I would have to go anyway, so I gave up.
<v Leo>我感到顺从。意思是，我知道无论如何我都要去，所以我放弃了。

00:58:06.380 --> 00:58:41.380
<v Sarah Koenig>Here's the curse of being almost 13-- old enough to understand his life will supposedly get better with time, but not old enough to really believe he's going to feel any differently than he does right this minute. Since making friends with Devin, Leo is one step toward being the kid he wants to be-- someone with pals, someone who's comfortable again. But he says right now, he's just worried he's going to be throwing up all year.
<v Sarah Koenig>这就是快13岁的诅咒——足够理解他的生活理应随着时间变得更好，但又不够大到真的相信他会感到和此时此刻有任何不同。自从和德文交朋友后，利奥离成为他想成为的孩子近了一步——一个有伙伴的孩子，一个再次感到舒服的孩子。但他现在说，他只是担心他会整年都在呕吐。

00:58:41.380 --> 00:58:57.700
<v David Kestenbaum>Sarah Koenig-- she's the host of Serial. In the years since this story first ran, Leo has made many friends. They've become immersed in the world of theater, and has finally forgiven their parents for the move. Leo did return to New York, though. They graduated from NYU this past spring.
<v David Kestenbaum>莎拉·柯尼希——她是《串行》（Serial）的主持人。在这个故事第一次播出后的几年里，利奥交了很多朋友。他们沉浸在戏剧世界中，终于原谅了父母的搬家。不过，利奥确实回到了纽约。他们今年春天从纽约大学毕业。

00:58:57.700 --> 00:59:01.900
<v David Kestenbaum>[MUSIC - "WE GOTTA GET OUT OF THIS PLACE" BY THE ANIMALS]
<v David Kestenbaum>[音乐 - 动物乐队（The Animals）的《我们得离开这个地方》（WE GOTTA GET OUT OF THIS PLACE）]

00:59:01.900 --> 00:59:17.330
<v David Kestenbaum>(SINGING) We gotta get out of this place if it's the last thing we ever do. We gotta get out of this place. 'Cause girl, there's a better life for me and you.
<v David Kestenbaum>（演唱）我们得离开这个地方，如果这是我们最后能做的事。我们得离开这个地方。因为女孩，有一个更好的生活等着我和你。

00:59:17.330 --> 00:59:47.960
<v David Kestenbaum>Today's program was produced by Diane Wu and Lina Misitzis. The people who put the show together today include Bim Adewunmi, Elna Baker, Ben Calhoun, Dana Chivvis, Sean Cole, Aviva DeKornfeld, Seth Lind, Stowe Nelson, Katherine Rae Mondo, Nadia Reiman, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, Nancy Updike, and Chloee Weiner. Our managing editor-- Sarah Abdurrahman. Our executive editor is Emanuele Berry. Special thanks today to Harrison Nesbit, Will Yurman, Hilary Appleman, and Maximo Berryman-Chace.
<v David Kestenbaum>今天的节目由黛安·吴和莉娜·米西齐斯制作。今天把节目拼凑在一起的人包括宾·阿德翁米、埃尔娜·贝克、本·卡尔霍恩、达纳·奇维斯、肖恩·科尔、阿维娃·德科恩菲尔德、塞斯·林德、斯托·纳尔逊、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多、纳迪亚·雷曼、克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉、马特·蒂尔尼、南希·厄普代克和克洛伊·温纳。我们的总编辑——莎拉·阿卜杜拉赫曼。我们的执行编辑是埃马努埃莱·贝里。今天特别感谢哈里森·内斯比特、威尔·尤尔曼、希拉里·阿普尔曼和马克西莫·贝里曼-蔡斯。

00:59:47.960 --> 01:00:02.120
<v David Kestenbaum>Our website-- thisamericanlife.org, where you can stream our archive of over 700 episodes for absolutely free. Again, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们的网站——thisamericanlife.org，在那里你可以免费流媒体播放我们超过700集的档案。再说一次，thisamericanlife.org。《美国生活》由公共广播交流中心PRX提供给公共广播电台。

01:00:02.120 --> 01:00:09.710
<v David Kestenbaum>Thanks to our boss, Ira Glass. You know, he says he's on vacation, but he keeps coming into the studio, making these tiny little edits to our stories.
<v David Kestenbaum>感谢我们的老板，艾拉·格拉斯。你知道，他说他在度假，但他一直在进录音室，对我们的故事做这些微小的编辑。

01:00:09.710 --> 01:00:14.240
<v Elna Baker>I'm really sorry. I'm sorry. I'm sorry I'm here. I'm sorry I'm doing this. I'm sorry.
<v Elna Baker>我真的很抱歉。对不起。对不起我在这里。对不起我做了这个。对不起。

01:00:14.240 --> 01:00:17.590
<v David Kestenbaum>We'll be back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们下周会带着更多《美国生活》的故事回来。

01:00:17.590 --> 01:00:22.590
<v David Kestenbaum>(SINGING) Girl, there's a better life for me and you. Somewhere, baby. Somehow, I know it, baby. We got to get out of this place if it's the last thing we ever do.
<v David Kestenbaum>（演唱）女孩，有一个更好的生活等着我和你。某个地方，宝贝。不知何故，我知道，宝贝。我们得离开这个地方，如果这是我们最后能做的事。
